var e=1,t={ts:{name:`TypeScript`,description:`From your first types to API models and React components.`},js:{name:`JavaScript`,description:`Language foundations, async work and behavior you can explain.`},react:{name:`React`,description:`State, Effects, identity, rendering and modern capabilities.`},web:{name:`Web foundations`,description:`HTML, CSS, accessibility, browser behavior, networking and security.`},stack:{name:`Libraries and data`,description:`Redux, Saga, Query, Router, Axios, forms, MUI and i18n.`},quality:{name:`Quality and tools`,description:`Tests, performance, Git, CI and production evidence.`},senior:{name:`Senior and team lead`,description:`Architecture, decisions, API contracts, mentoring and ownership.`},ai:{name:`AI coding tools`,description:`Codex and Claude Code: context, verification, boundaries and independence.`},vite:{name:`Vite and build tools`,description:`Development server, builds, environment values, assets and reliable delivery.`},cra:{name:`CRA and react-scripts`,description:`Legacy maintenance, scripts, limitations and deliberate migration.`},node:{name:`Node.js foundations`,description:`Runtime, modules, asynchronous I/O, errors, streams and server operations.`},express:{name:`Express.js`,description:`Routes, middleware, validation, errors and HTTP API boundaries.`},sass:{name:`Sass / SCSS`,description:`Preprocessing, modules, tokens, nesting and maintainable styles.`},less:{name:`Less`,description:`Variables, mixins, guards, arithmetic and the build/browser boundary.`},ui:{name:`UI libraries and Figma`,description:`MUI, Bootstrap, design tokens, and developer handoff.`},viz:{name:`Charts and maps`,description:`Chart.js, accessible data, and Google Maps integration.`}},n=JSON.parse(`{"ts01":{"title":"What does TypeScript actually check?","prompt":"An API returns data with the wrong shape. Does TypeScript automatically stop it in the browser?","why":"TypeScript checks your program before it runs. An annotation on an API response does not inspect the actual JSON.","pitfall":"A type annotation is not a runtime validator.","options":["No. Static types are erased; external data needs runtime validation.","Yes, if I declare the response as Promise<User>.","Yes, if I enable strict.","Yes, if I use interface instead of type."],"steps":["The compiler can see your source and type declarations during development. It cannot control what a remote server sends tomorrow.","If the server sends name: null, a runtime validator must catch that before you call name.toUpperCase()."],"remember":"A type annotation is not a runtime validator.","wrong":["Promise<User> describes an expected result without inspecting the response.","strict adds static checks, not an automatic browser validator.","Interfaces are erased from the emitted JavaScript too."]},"ts02":{"title":"Inference instead of excessive annotations","prompt":"What is the type of the variable after this declaration?","why":"With let count = 3, the numeric literal is normally widened to number because the value can change.","pitfall":"Inference determines a type; it does not disable checking.","code":"let count = 3;","options":["number","Only the literal number 3","any","string | number"],"steps":["A let binding can later hold another number. Restricting it to exactly 3 would usually be too narrow.","Assigning \\"4\\" is still an error: allowing the value to change does not allow every possible type."],"remember":"Inference determines a type; it does not disable checking.","wrong":["The literal type 3 permits only that value; this let declaration normally widens it.","There is enough information to infer number without any.","No string value or explicit union is present in this declaration."]},"ts03":{"title":"Type a small function","prompt":"Write formatPrice. It accepts a number and an optional currency string defaulting to RSD, and returns a string.","why":"Types document the input and output. A default value handles an omitted argument, but does not validate a currency code.","pitfall":"Type the function boundary; ordinary JavaScript still processes the values.","solution":"function formatPrice(amount: number, currency = \\"RSD\\"): string {\\n  return \`\${amount.toFixed(2)} \${currency}\`;\\n}","checks":["amount is typed as number.","currency has a default value, or undefined is handled.","Every return path produces a string; inferred return types are valid too."],"steps":["amount: number defines the input contract. currency = \\"RSD\\" supplies both a default value and string inference.","toFixed returns a string. A template literal constructs the final text; an explicit return annotation can document the public contract."],"remember":"Type the function boundary; ordinary JavaScript still processes the values.","wrong":[]},"ts04":{"title":"any or unknown?","prompt":"Which starting type requires you to check an unknown external value before using it?","why":"unknown accepts any value, but requires narrowing before type-specific operations.","pitfall":"unknown requires evidence before use.","options":["unknown","any","never","object in every case"],"steps":["The value might be a number, null, a string, or an object, so calling a string method immediately is unsafe.","A typeof check provides evidence that a particular branch may use string operations."],"remember":"unknown requires evidence before use.","wrong":["any generally allows operations without the protection required here.","never represents an impossible value, not an unknown input.","object does not describe all possible inputs or validate their structure."]},"ts05":{"title":"Optional properties and undefined","prompt":"With strictNullChecks enabled, what is the type of user.name when read?","why":"The ? allows the property to be absent. Check it or supply an appropriate fallback before using it as a string.","pitfall":"Optional data requires a decision about absence.","code":"type User = { id: string; name?: string };\\ndeclare const user: User;","options":["string | undefined","Only string","Only null","never"],"steps":["{ id: \\"1\\" } is a valid User in this example. Reading its absent name property returns undefined.","Use a check or user.name ?? \\"No name\\" according to the intended behavior. A non-null assertion does not create a missing value."],"remember":"Optional data requires a decision about absence.","wrong":["string alone would incorrectly promise that a name always exists.","null is not included unless explicitly declared.","never represents impossibility, not an absent optional property."]},"ts06":{"title":"Unions in everyday code","prompt":"Write normalizeId(id: string | number): string without any or a type assertion.","why":"A union allows one of several types. String(id) is sufficient for these two supported inputs.","pitfall":"Conversion changes a value; assertion changes an assumption.","solution":"function normalizeId(id: string | number): string {\\n  return String(id);\\n}","checks":["The input accepts string | number.","Both supported cases return a string.","There is no unnecessary as or any."],"steps":["String(id) performs a real conversion. A type assertion would only change the compiler’s assumption.","A typeof branch is also possible, but adds no necessary behavior to this simple conversion."],"remember":"Conversion changes a value; assertion changes an assumption.","wrong":[]},"ts07":{"title":"type and interface","prompt":"Which statement is correct?","why":"Both are useful for many object and props contracts. Choose by the capabilities and conventions you need.","pitfall":"Choose a model for its contract, not a universal style slogan.","options":["Both can describe objects; type can represent unions and interface supports declaration merging.","An interface is a browser runtime validator.","type always provides weaker object checking.","An interface can directly alias string | number."],"steps":["An ordinary props object can be modeled effectively with either form. The differences matter when you need specific features.","A union of primitives naturally uses a type alias; interfaces support extension and declaration merging."],"remember":"Choose a model for its contract, not a universal style slogan.","wrong":["An interface does not survive as executable validation code.","An object type alias is not inherently less safe.","An interface describes object structure rather than directly aliasing a primitive union."]},"ts08":{"title":"Narrowing from unknown","prompt":"Write getLabel(value: unknown): string. Trim a string input; otherwise return an empty string.","why":"Narrowing connects an actual JavaScript condition to a more precise static type in a branch.","pitfall":"Check first, then use the type-specific operation.","solution":"function getLabel(value: unknown): string {\\n  return typeof value === \\"string\\" ? value.trim() : \\"\\";\\n}","checks":["The input is unknown.","A typeof check precedes string-specific operations.","All paths return a string."],"steps":["Before the check, value might not support trim.","The string branch establishes that trim is safe; the other branch supplies the agreed fallback."],"remember":"Check first, then use the type-specific operation.","wrong":[]},"ts09":{"title":"Null and objects","prompt":"Why is this condition insufficient for reading value.id safely?","why":"Exclude null, check the property, and check its type. An object check is not a schema check.","pitfall":"An object is not automatically an object of the required shape.","code":"function read(value: unknown) {\\n  if (typeof value === \\"object\\") {\\n    // value.id ?\\n  }\\n}","options":["typeof null is \\"object\\", and an arbitrary object may not have id.","typeof cannot be used on unknown.","Every object is automatically a User.","It becomes sufficient when strict is enabled."],"steps":["JavaScript returns \\"object\\" for null for historical reasons. Reading a property from null throws.","An object such as { other: 1 } also passes this condition without providing id."],"remember":"An object is not automatically an object of the required shape.","wrong":["typeof on unknown is valid and is a standard narrowing operation.","Being an object does not establish the User contract.","strict can expose the problem; it cannot make an incomplete condition validate more data."]},"ts10":{"title":"Model request state","prompt":"Create a discriminated union RequestState<T> with idle, loading, success and error. Only success has data; only error has message.","why":"Separate union members exclude combinations that independent boolean flags can accidentally allow.","pitfall":"Model valid states instead of repeatedly repairing invalid combinations.","solution":"type RequestState<T> =\\n  | { status: \\"idle\\" }\\n  | { status: \\"loading\\" }\\n  | { status: \\"success\\"; data: T }\\n  | { status: \\"error\\"; message: string };","checks":["Each member has a shared status property with a distinct literal.","success includes data: T and error includes message: string.","The type does not allow success without data."],"steps":["Several flags can describe contradictory states, such as success with no data.","A status check narrows to the appropriate member and makes its specific fields available."],"remember":"Model valid states instead of repeatedly repairing invalid combinations.","wrong":[]},"ts11":{"title":"Exhaustiveness with never","prompt":"How can adding a union member produce a type error in an incomplete switch?","why":"After every valid member has been handled, nothing remains. A new unhandled member is no longer never.","pitfall":"A new union member should reveal the places that still need handling.","solution":"function assertNever(value: never): never {\\n  throw new Error(\\"Unexpected state\\");\\n}\\n// In the default branch: return assertNever(state);","checks":["Use a discriminated union and handle its existing members.","Pass the remaining value to a function accepting never.","Distinguish compile-time checking from runtime protection."],"steps":["In the exhaustive branch, the remaining value should be impossible.","Adding a member makes that value possible again, so assertNever detects a missing case."],"remember":"A new union member should reveal the places that still need handling.","wrong":[]},"ts12":{"title":"Generics preserve relationships","prompt":"What does first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined preserve better than any[]?","why":"A generic carries type information. undefined remains necessary because the array may be empty.","pitfall":"A generic carries the concrete type through a contract.","options":["The relationship between the array element type and the return type.","A guarantee that the array is nonempty.","Runtime validation of API data.","Faster browser execution in every case."],"steps":["With number[], T is number; with User[], T is User. The result keeps that relationship.","Using any loses useful information and permits incorrect operations without warnings."],"remember":"A generic carries the concrete type through a contract.","wrong":["T[] still permits an empty array.","Generic parameters are not runtime validators.","Generics are erased and do not inherently improve execution speed."]},"ts13":{"title":"A generic getProperty","prompt":"Write getProperty accepting an object and one of its keys, returning the precise property type.","why":"keyof describes permitted keys; T[K] describes the value associated with a chosen key.","pitfall":"Connect the object, the allowed key, and the resulting value type.","solution":"function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}","checks":["Use T and K extends keyof T.","Return T[K], explicitly or through inference.","Do not accept an arbitrary string as a guaranteed existing key."],"steps":["T retains the concrete object type and K retains the particular key.","The return type can distinguish a numeric age from a string name instead of discarding that precision."],"remember":"Connect the object, the allowed key, and the resulting value type.","wrong":[]},"ts14":{"title":"A generic constraint","prompt":"You need to read id while retaining the input’s additional fields. Which signature fits?","why":"A constraint specifies the minimum shape the implementation needs, while T retains the concrete input type.","pitfall":"A constraint is the minimum; T retains the specific shape.","options":["function keep<T extends { id: string }>(value: T): T","function keep(value: any): never","function keep<T>(value: T): string in every case","function keep(value: { id: string }): unknown"],"steps":["The implementation may read id because every permitted T provides it.","Returning the original value preserves extra properties. Returning only a minimal object would not justify promising T."],"remember":"A constraint is the minimum; T retains the specific shape.","wrong":["never promises no normal return, which is not the intended behavior.","An unconstrained T does not guarantee id, and string does not retain the object.","unknown discards information about the returned fields."]},"ts15":{"title":"Pick and Omit","prompt":"Which type retains only id and name from User?","why":"Pick selects named keys. Omit excludes keys and Partial makes all properties optional.","pitfall":"Pick selects; Omit excludes.","options":["Pick<User, \\"id\\" | \\"name\\">","Omit<User, \\"id\\" | \\"name\\">","Partial<User>","Record<User, \\"id\\">"],"steps":["The selected property types come from User, avoiding duplicated definitions.","Choose a separate request model when the endpoint has different business rules."],"remember":"Pick selects; Omit excludes.","wrong":["Omit removes precisely the two properties requested here.","Partial retains all properties while making them optional.","Record expects a set of keys and a value type; User is not that key set here."]},"ts16":{"title":"Partial and business rules","prompt":"Why is Partial<User> not always suitable for an update request? Propose a type for changing name and phone.","why":"A request type should describe the operation’s allowed changes rather than expose the entire entity.","pitfall":"Model a business operation, not the whole database entity.","solution":"type UpdateProfile = Partial<Pick<User, \\"name\\" | \\"phone\\">>;\\n// Define deletion separately if the endpoint uses null.","checks":["Do not permit editing id, role, or protected fields.","Select allowed fields before making them optional.","Distinguish omitted fields, null and empty strings according to the API."],"steps":["User may contain fields the profile endpoint must never let the caller change.","Partial<Pick<User, \\"name\\" | \\"phone\\">> narrows the editable surface. Server authorization remains necessary."],"remember":"Model a business operation, not the whole database entity.","wrong":[]},"ts17":{"title":"as const does not freeze runtime data","prompt":"What does as const do here?","why":"A const assertion affects static types. Runtime immutability is a separate concern.","pitfall":"Static readonly and runtime freezing are different mechanisms.","code":"const statuses = [\\"draft\\", \\"sent\\"] as const;","options":["Preserves literal types and a readonly view; it does not deeply freeze runtime data.","Calls Object.freeze recursively.","Converts the array into a Set.","Disables every type check."],"steps":["The assertion preserves the exact status literals instead of widening them to arbitrary strings.","Readonly typing does not insert code that prevents mutation through every possible runtime path."],"remember":"Static readonly and runtime freezing are different mechanisms.","wrong":["No Object.freeze call is inserted; ordinary Object.freeze is not recursive either.","The runtime value remains an array.","This increases type precision rather than disabling checking."]},"ts18":{"title":"satisfies and inference","prompt":"Why use satisfies rather than a broad annotation or an assertion?","why":"satisfies can validate configuration contracts while retaining useful information about the expression. Context can still influence inference.","pitfall":"Check the contract while preserving useful precision.","options":["It checks compatibility without replacing the expression’s inferred type with the broad target type.","It validates server responses at runtime.","It always makes the value immutable.","It turns every number into a numeric literal type."],"steps":["A broad annotation may hide useful precision, while an assertion can make an unjustified promise.","satisfies checks the required relationship and lets the expression retain its resulting type."],"remember":"Check the contract while preserving useful precision.","wrong":["It does not inspect future network responses.","It does not inherently imply readonly or runtime immutability.","Literal inference depends on context; satisfies is not a universal replacement for as const."]},"ts19":{"title":"Mapped and conditional types","prompt":"Explain ReadonlyFields<T> and ElementOf<T>. Implement simple versions and give a use case.","why":"These tools help with reusable models and library APIs. Avoid making ordinary props unnecessarily complex.","pitfall":"Type transformations do not iterate over runtime objects.","solution":"type ReadonlyFields<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };\\ntype ElementOf<T> = T extends readonly (infer U)[] ? U : never;\\n\\ntype User = { id: number; profile: { name: string } };\\ntype ReadonlyUser = ReadonlyFields<User>;\\ntype ListItem = ElementOf<User[]>; // User\\nconst view: ReadonlyUser = { id: 1, profile: { name: \\"Ada\\" } };\\n// view.id = 2; // Type error: readonly field.\\nview.profile.name = \\"Ana\\"; // Allowed: this readonly mapping is shallow.","checks":["A mapped type iterates over keyof T.","A conditional type uses extends and infer to extract an array element.","These are static type transformations.","Shows a concrete model and distinguishes shallow readonly from deep immutability."],"steps":["A mapped type systematically changes the properties of an existing type, such as adding readonly.","A conditional type chooses a result based on a type relationship; infer U captures a matched element type."],"remember":"Type transformations do not iterate over runtime objects.","wrong":[]},"ts20":{"title":"React props and callbacks","prompt":"Type SaveButton with label, disabled and onSave. onSave accepts id: string and returns void.","why":"A props type documents what the component accepts and what the caller must provide.","pitfall":"Callback parameters are part of a component’s public API.","solution":"type SaveButtonProps = {\\n  label: string;\\n  disabled?: boolean;\\n  onSave: (id: string) => void;\\n};","checks":["label is string and disabled is an optional boolean.","The callback type is (id: string) => void.","The component and caller follow the same props contract."],"steps":["The caller must supply the required label and a compatible callback.","The callback describes information passed back to the parent; disabled can be omitted."],"remember":"Callback parameters are part of a component’s public API.","wrong":[]},"ts21":{"title":"useState with null","prompt":"Which declaration allows an initial null followed by a User?","why":"An explicit union describes future states that the initial null value cannot reveal by itself.","pitfall":"An initial value does not always describe every future state.","options":["useState<User | null>(null)","useState(null) without another type always works","useState<User>(null) with strictNullChecks","useState<any>(null) is the only option"],"steps":["The compiler cannot infer a future User solely from the initial null.","When reading user.name, first narrow away null so the type remains honest during loading."],"remember":"An initial value does not always describe every future state.","wrong":["This normally infers null, making a later User incompatible.","With strictNullChecks, null is not User unless included.","any abandons useful protection; an explicit union expresses the real contract."]},"ts22":{"title":"Input events without any","prompt":"Write a typed React onChange handler for an HTML input and read its string value.","why":"DOM input value remains a string. Numeric data needs explicit conversion and handling of empty or invalid values.","pitfall":"Typing an event does not convert its value.","solution":"function handleChange(event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {\\n  setName(event.currentTarget.value);\\n}","checks":["Use React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement> or JSX handler inference.","Read event.currentTarget.value.","Do not assume type=\\"number\\" makes value a number."],"steps":["ChangeEvent describes the React event and HTMLInputElement identifies the concrete element.","currentTarget is the element with this handler. Its value is still text, even for a numeric input."],"remember":"Typing an event does not convert its value.","wrong":[]},"ts23":{"title":"Props that exclude invalid states","prompt":"Model an Alert with success or error variants. Only error requires a retry callback.","why":"Discriminated props allow natural narrowing and rule out some invalid combinations at call sites.","pitfall":"Describe valid combinations, not just individual properties.","solution":"type AlertProps =\\n  | { variant: \\"success\\"; message: string; retry?: never }\\n  | { variant: \\"error\\"; message: string; retry: () => void };","checks":["Use union members with literal variants.","The error member requires retry.","Do not require retry for success; understand structural typing limits."],"steps":["Making retry optional everywhere would permit an error variant without its required recovery action.","Separate members describe valid combinations. retry?: never further restricts success, with optional-property details depending on TS settings."],"remember":"Describe valid combinations, not just individual properties.","wrong":[]},"ts24":{"title":"An API response is not a promised type","prompt":"Explain the problem and propose a safer flow.","why":"External data is outside your compiler’s control. Validate and normalize it at the system boundary.","pitfall":"Successful HTTP and valid business data are different claims.","code":"const user = await response.json() as User;\\nreturn user.name.toUpperCase();","solution":"const response = await fetch(\\"/api/me\\");\\nif (!response.ok) throw new Error(\`HTTP \${response.status}\`);\\nconst raw: unknown = await response.json();\\nconst user = UserSchema.parse(raw); // with a defined schema","checks":["as User is not runtime validation.","Check the HTTP status and JSON shape separately.","Use validation and handle invalid responses."],"steps":["JSON can parse successfully without containing any required User properties. An assertion cannot repair that.","An HTTP status check and a schema check establish different facts and can fail independently."],"remember":"Successful HTTP and valid business data are different claims.","wrong":[]},"ts25":{"title":"A bundler is not a type checker","prompt":"A bundler strips TS syntax and builds the app successfully. Does this prove there are no type errors?","why":"Transpilation, type checking and behavior tests detect different problems.","pitfall":"Build, type-check and test are distinct checks.","options":["No. Run a type-check step such as tsc --noEmit according to the project configuration.","Yes, every bundler checks all types.","Yes, if the browser console has no errors.","No, TypeScript can never check React."],"steps":["A transpiler can emit runnable JavaScript despite type mismatches.","A dedicated check in CI detects those mismatches; tests then cover behavior outside the type system."],"remember":"Build, type-check and test are distinct checks.","wrong":["Actual behavior depends on the bundler and build script.","One successful execution path does not prove every type relationship is valid.","React and TypeScript work together with suitable configuration and checking."]},"ts26":{"title":"Migrate a JavaScript project to TypeScript","prompt":"You have a large React/Redux project and a feature deadline. Propose an incremental migration.","why":"The goal is safer development. Renaming files while adding any everywhere can create cost without protection.","pitfall":"Measure migration by better guarantees, not just .tsx file counts.","solution":"Agree on strictness → type one API module → complete one vertical feature → check regressions → expand incrementally.","checks":["Start with boundaries, shared models, and new or modified modules.","Add type checking to CI and reduce any with explicit priorities.","Use small PRs, compatibility checks, tests, and measurable phase completion."],"steps":["A large rewrite makes migration failures harder to separate from feature changes.","A small vertical slice validates API models and UI together before the pattern spreads."],"remember":"Measure migration by better guarantees, not just .tsx file counts.","wrong":[]},"ts27":{"title":"A typed asynchronous service","prompt":"Design getUsers(): Promise<User[]>. Where do HTTP errors, invalid JSON, cancellation and UI errors belong?","why":"Promise<User[]> describes a successful result. Its signature alone does not verify every failure path.","pitfall":"Do not hide failure as an empty successful result.","solution":"async function getUsers(signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<User[]> {\\n  const r = await fetch(\\"/api/users\\", { signal });\\n  if (!r.ok) throw new Error(\`HTTP \${r.status}\`);\\n  return UsersSchema.parse(await r.json());\\n}","checks":["Separate network/HTTP, parsing and schema validation.","Accept or forward AbortSignal and distinguish cancellation from ordinary failure.","Model loading, empty, error and success; do not conceal failure by returning []."],"steps":["The network, status, body format and execution time can each fail outside the return type’s promise.","The service checks data boundaries while the UI presents meaningful states and recovery."],"remember":"Do not hide failure as an empty successful result.","wrong":[]},"ts28":{"title":"TypeScript capstone: a typed table","prompt":"Design a read-only Column<T> with stable id, title and render(row). Add shared sorting for desktop rows and mobile cards.","why":"A senior solution connects type contracts, behavior and usability. A generic alone cannot guarantee correct sorting.","pitfall":"Types connect contracts; shared implementation preserves behavior.","solution":"type Column<T> = {\\n  id: string;\\n  title: string;\\n  render: (row: T) => React.ReactNode;\\n  getSortValue?: (row: T) => string | number | null;\\n};\\n// Define null ordering, locale rules, and stability.","checks":["The generic binds row to T and gives render an appropriate output type.","Separate display values from sort values.","Share sort rules and sort the full client-side set before pagination."],"steps":["Column<T> lets the compiler detect reads of properties that the row type does not provide.","A formatted currency string may display correctly but sort incorrectly. Shared source values and comparators preserve consistency."],"remember":"Types connect contracts; shared implementation preserves behavior.","wrong":[]},"js01":{"title":"const does not mean immutable","prompt":"What happens?","why":"The binding remains unchanged while the referenced object is modified.","pitfall":"A constant reference does not imply immutable contents.","code":"const u = { count: 1 };\\nu.count = 2;\\nconsole.log(u.count);","options":["It prints 2. const prevents rebinding, not property mutation.","It throws at u.count = 2.","It prints 1.","The object becomes deeply frozen."],"steps":["const fixes which value the name refers to; it does not freeze the object’s contents.","u.count = 2 changes the object. Assigning a different object to u would attempt forbidden rebinding."],"remember":"A constant reference does not imply immutable contents.","wrong":["The property is not automatically readonly or frozen.","The assignment happens before the value is logged.","const does not call Object.freeze."]},"js02":{"title":"Event loop and microtasks","prompt":"In a browser, what is the output order for this standalone example?","why":"Synchronous code finishes first, then the Promise microtask runs before the next timer task.","pitfall":"For this example: synchronous code → microtasks → next task.","code":"console.log(\\"A\\");\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"B\\"), 0);\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"C\\"));\\nconsole.log(\\"D\\");","options":["A, D, C, B","A, B, C, D","A, C, D, B","A, D, B, C"],"steps":["A and D run on the current synchronous execution path. Neither callback interrupts it.","The microtask queue is drained next, producing C, before the timer task produces B."],"remember":"For this example: synchronous code → microtasks → next task.","wrong":["Scheduling a timer does not run its callback immediately.","The Promise callback does not interrupt the remaining synchronous log.","The Promise microtask runs before this timer callback."]},"js03":{"title":"Closures in practice","prompt":"Explain a closure using makeCounter. Why can two makeCounter calls have independent counters?","why":"A closure provides access to the environment where a function was created, rather than automatically snapshotting every value.","pitfall":"Identify which lexical bindings are actually shared.","solution":"function makeCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n  return () => ++count;\\n}\\nconst a = makeCounter();\\nconst b = makeCounter();\\n// a(): 1, a(): 2, b(): 1","checks":["A function retains access to its lexical environment.","Each factory call creates a separate environment.","Give an example and connect the idea to stale closures in React."],"steps":["The returned function can still access count after makeCounter has returned.","Separate factory calls create separate bindings; functions from the same environment may instead share a binding."],"remember":"Identify which lexical bindings are actually shared.","wrong":[]},"js04":{"title":"Spread makes a shallow copy","prompt":"What is a.address.city after this code?","why":"Only the outer object is copied. Both address properties still reference the same nested object.","pitfall":"Spread does not deeply copy the reference graph.","code":"const a = { address: { city: \\"Beograd\\" } };\\nconst b = { ...a };\\nb.address.city = \\"Novi Sad\\";","options":["Novi Sad","Beograd","undefined","The code always throws."],"steps":["a and b are different outer objects but share one address object.","Changing b.address.city is therefore visible through a.address.city. Copy the changed nested branch for an independent update."],"remember":"Spread does not deeply copy the reference graph.","wrong":["Beograd would remain if the nested address had been independently copied.","city exists and receives a new value.","Mutating this ordinary, unfrozen object is allowed."]},"js05":{"title":"?? and || are not interchangeable","prompt":"Which expression preserves an intentional value of 0?","why":"?? falls back only for null or undefined. || also falls back for 0, false and an empty string.","pitfall":"Choose between missing and falsy values deliberately.","code":"const count = 0;","options":["count ?? 10","count || 10","Both always return 10","Neither can return a number."],"steps":["Zero can be meaningful business data, such as an empty count or a zero price.","|| treats zero as falsy; ?? tests absence rather than general falsiness."],"remember":"Choose between missing and falsy values deliberately.","wrong":["0 is falsy, so || returns the fallback 10.","The operators use different conditions for choosing a fallback.","Both may return numbers; the difference is which operand they select."]},"js06":{"title":"Promise.all or allSettled?","prompt":"Three independent widgets load data and one endpoint fails. When would you use each method?","why":"Starting requests, coordinating their results, and cancelling them are separate decisions.","pitfall":"Result coordination is not execution cancellation.","solution":"// all: every input is required for one result.\\n// allSettled: handle fulfilled/rejected outcomes per widget.","checks":["all rejects when an input rejects; it does not automatically cancel other work.","allSettled provides the outcome of each input.","Choose according to whether a partial result is useful."],"steps":["Use a combined failure when the result is only meaningful if all required inputs succeed.","For independent widgets, retain successful results and display local failures for the others."],"remember":"Result coordination is not execution cancellation.","wrong":[]},"js07":{"title":"Debounce a search","prompt":"Write a small debounce. Explain cleanup and why debounce does not prevent out-of-order API results.","why":"Debouncing reduces how often work starts. Requests already sent can still complete out of order.","pitfall":"Debounce controls starts; race protection controls which result is applied.","solution":"function debounce(fn, ms) {\\n  let timer;\\n  function run(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timer);\\n    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);\\n  }\\n  run.cancel = () => clearTimeout(timer);\\n  return run;\\n}\\n// This minimal version does not forward dynamic this.","checks":["Cancel the previous timer and schedule the latest call.","Preserve arguments and support cancelling the timer.","Handle request races separately."],"steps":["Every new call replaces the previous timer, retaining the latest arguments.","Removing a timer does not remove a request already in flight. Guard against applying its stale result."],"remember":"Debounce controls starts; race protection controls which result is applied.","wrong":[]},"js08":{"title":"this and arrow functions","prompt":"Compare an object method and an arrow function when passed as callbacks. How does bind help?","why":"An arrow function is not always a better method. The intended receiver determines the appropriate choice.","pitfall":"Inspect both the call form and where the function was created.","solution":"const safeCallback = object.method.bind(object);\\n// ili () => object.method()","checks":["A regular function gets this from its call form.","An arrow function captures this from its environment.","An extracted method can lose its receiver; bind or a wrapper can preserve it."],"steps":["obj.method() supplies obj as receiver; an extracted callback call may not.","bind creates a function with a bound this. An arrow does not get a new this simply because it is called as a method."],"remember":"Inspect both the call form and where the function was created.","wrong":[]},"js09":{"title":"Sorting numbers","prompt":"Which sorts numbers ascending while preserving the original array?","why":"Default sort compares string representations. sort mutates its array; copying protects the input.","pitfall":"Use a valid comparator and an explicit mutation policy.","options":["[...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b)","numbers.sort()","numbers.sort((a, b) => a > b)","numbers.reverse()"],"steps":["String ordering can place 10 before 2.","a - b expresses a negative, zero, or positive numeric relationship. The copied array is the one being sorted."],"remember":"Use a valid comparator and an explicit mutation policy.","wrong":["This mutates the input and does not request numeric comparison.","A boolean comparator does not express both comparison directions correctly.","reverse only reverses the existing order."]},"js10":{"title":"Search and stale responses","prompt":"A user types Ana, then Anja. The Ana response arrives last. Prevent it from replacing the newer result.","why":"The latest user intention should determine the UI, not whichever response arrives last.","pitfall":"The latest intention wins, not the last arriving packet.","solution":"let latest = 0;\\nasync function search(q) {\\n  const requestId = ++latest;\\n  const data = await load(q);\\n  if (requestId === latest) show(data);\\n}\\n// Add error and cancellation handling for the environment.","checks":["Identify the race condition.","Use AbortController and/or verify the latest request identity.","Handle cancellation and failures distinctly when the parameter changes."],"steps":["Arrival time does not identify the active user intention.","Before applying a response, confirm it belongs to the current request; cancellation separately reduces unnecessary work."],"remember":"The latest intention wins, not the last arriving packet.","wrong":[]},"js11":{"title":"Map, Set and complexity","prompt":"Join 10,000 rows by id. Why can an index be preferable to calling find inside map?","why":"Building one lookup index and then joining often improves both clarity and efficiency.","pitfall":"Choose a data structure for the access pattern.","solution":"const byId = new Map(otherRows.map(row => [row.id, row]));\\nconst merged = rows.map(row => ({ ...row, extra: byId.get(row.id) }));\\n// The last duplicate id wins here; define the intended rule.","checks":["Recognize repeated linear searches and possible O(n*m) work.","Build an index and define missing/duplicate id behavior.","Do not present expected Map lookup cost as an unconditional guarantee."],"steps":["find scans the second array repeatedly for each row in the first.","An index avoids repeated scans, but duplicate keys and unmatched rows still need explicit rules."],"remember":"Choose a data structure for the access pattern.","wrong":[]},"js12":{"title":"Modules and tree shaking","prompt":"Explain ESM import/export, dynamic import, and why tree shaking cannot remove every unused-looking statement.","why":"A smaller initial bundle can help, but too many small lazy chunks can create a waterfall.","pitfall":"Deferred loading has network and timing costs.","solution":"const { openEditor } = await import(\\"./editor.js\\");\\nopenEditor();","checks":["Distinguish static modules from asynchronous import().","Account for side effects and bundler capabilities.","Evaluate splitting against network cost and user flow."],"steps":["Static module relationships help the bundler reason about dependencies. import() enables deferred loading.","Code with observable side effects may need to remain. Inspect the actual output rather than assuming all unused-looking code disappears."],"remember":"Deferred loading has network and timing costs.","wrong":[]},"re01":{"title":"State is a snapshot","prompt":"Starting with count = 0, a click runs this handler. What is the next count?","why":"Both expressions use count from the same render. Use functional updaters for two successive increments.","pitfall":"Snapshot values differ from queued updater functions.","code":"setCount(count + 1);\\nsetCount(count + 1);","options":["1","2","0","It depends only on browser speed."],"steps":["The handler sees count as 0, so both calls request the same next value: 1.","Two setCount(c => c + 1) calls compose updates through the queue instead."],"remember":"Snapshot values differ from queued updater functions.","wrong":["Two requests for the same value 1 do not automatically become two increments.","A state change is scheduled and a later render receives it.","The handler’s semantics do not depend on browser speed."]},"re02":{"title":"Derived values without an Effect","prompt":"First and last names are already state. Where should fullName be calculated?","why":"A derived value generally needs no separate state. An Effect adds another pass and possible inconsistency.","pitfall":"Do not store what you can simply derive from existing state.","options":["During render, from firstName and lastName.","In separate state with a mandatory Effect.","In a global store in every case.","By directly changing the DOM."],"steps":["fullName holds no independent information beyond its two inputs.","Keeping another state value creates another source that must be synchronized."],"remember":"Do not store what you can simply derive from existing state.","wrong":["The mandatory Effect introduces unnecessary synchronization.","A global store does not solve redundant derived state.","Manual DOM changes bypass React’s rendering model without a need."]},"re03":{"title":"Keys and identity","prompt":"Why can using an index as key break inputs when rows are reordered?","why":"This is about correctness as well as performance: state may follow the wrong business item.","pitfall":"A key should follow the data rather than its current position.","solution":"{rows.map(row => <Row key={row.id} row={row} />)}","checks":["Keys help match element identity across renders.","A positional key can attach state to the wrong row.","Use stable data ids rather than random keys generated during render."],"steps":["React uses element type and identity, including sibling keys, when matching renders.","A stable id follows the row when its position changes; an index follows the position."],"remember":"A key should follow the data rather than its current position.","wrong":[]},"re04":{"title":"Effects as synchronization","prompt":"How do you choose dependencies and cleanup for a subscription or event listener?","why":"A dependency array is not an arbitrary schedule independent of the Effect’s code.","pitfall":"Every setup needs a corresponding resource-cleanup story.","solution":"useEffect(() => {\\n  const unsubscribe = subscribe(roomId, handleMessage);\\n  return unsubscribe;\\n}, [roomId, handleMessage]);","checks":["The Effect synchronizes with an external system.","List the reactive values it reads.","Cleanup removes the previous connection and supports reconnecting."],"steps":["Setup connects using the values of that render, so changed values may require a new connection.","Cleanup removes the previous connection before appropriate replacement or unmounting."],"remember":"Every setup needs a corresponding resource-cleanup story.","wrong":[]},"re05":{"title":"Strict Mode during development","prompt":"What does an additional development setup → cleanup → setup cycle check?","why":"Development checks expose resilience problems. Correct synchronization is the fix.","pitfall":"Fix reconnecting instead of bypassing the check.","options":["Whether synchronization and cleanup handle reconnecting correctly.","Every Effect must run twice in production.","A ref flag makes cleanup unnecessary.","API operations automatically become idempotent."],"steps":["A duplicate listener or leaked resource becomes easier to notice with an extra cycle.","Blocking the next setup can hide the bug and leave the component disconnected after cleanup."],"remember":"Fix reconnecting instead of bypassing the check.","wrong":["A development check does not define universal production duplication.","A ref flag is not a replacement for resource management.","Server idempotency requires a separate API contract."]},"re06":{"title":"State or ref?","prompt":"Where would you store a displayed input value and a timer id, and why?","why":"Changing ref.current does not itself request a render. State and refs solve different needs.","pitfall":"Should changing this value change the screen?","solution":"const [query, setQuery] = useState(\\"\\");\\nconst timerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);","checks":["Use state when a change should affect rendering.","Use a ref for a retained mutable value that does not need a render.","Do not use render-time ref mutation as business synchronization."],"steps":["The input value is visual data, so changes should update the UI.","A timer id is retained to cancel work; changing that id is not itself a visual update."],"remember":"Should changing this value change the screen?","wrong":[]},"re07":{"title":"Memoization and React Compiler","prompt":"How do you choose between simple code, memo/useMemo/useCallback, and compiler optimization?","why":"Compiler can automatically memoize supported React code at build time, while state and purity still matter.","pitfall":"Confirm optimization against the actual measured bottleneck.","solution":"Base the answer on a profiler trace, data size, and the specific project configuration.","checks":["Measure and distinguish rendering from DOM changes.","Understand prop identity and memoization costs.","Check whether Compiler is configured; it does not solve network or architecture problems."],"steps":["Memoization reuses work when relevant inputs remain the same; rapidly changing inputs can reduce its usefulness.","Compiler automates some of that work, but does not shrink API responses or replace a poor algorithm."],"remember":"Confirm optimization against the actual measured bottleneck.","wrong":[]},"re08":{"title":"Context and rendering","prompt":"Which is a reasonable statement about Context?","why":"Context distributes values through a tree. Update frequency and consumption granularity still need design.","pitfall":"Separate responsibilities before adding memoization everywhere.","options":["Provider value changes can update consumers; state placement and value identity matter.","Context prevents every re-render automatically.","Context fully replaces a server cache.","Every global value needs a separate Redux store."],"steps":["A consumer reads its nearest provider’s value and may update when that value changes.","Combining unrelated changing values can affect more consumers than intended."],"remember":"Separate responsibilities before adding memoization everywhere.","wrong":["Context distributes values rather than universally blocking renders.","Server caching includes freshness and invalidation beyond Context alone.","Sharing a value does not automatically require another store."]},"re09":{"title":"Controlled and uncontrolled forms","prompt":"Explain both approaches with an input. What are the tradeoffs for large forms and UI libraries?","why":"The right choice depends on behavior and tooling, not a universal hierarchy of professionalism.","pitfall":"Choose field ownership for the behavior you need.","solution":"import { useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nexport function InputExamples() {\\n  const [name, setName] = useState(\\"\\");\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <label>Controlled\\n        <input value={name} onChange={event => setName(event.currentTarget.value)} />\\n      </label>\\n      <form onSubmit={event => {\\n        event.preventDefault();\\n        const value = new FormData(event.currentTarget).get(\\"name\\");\\n        console.log(value);\\n      }}>\\n        <label>Uncontrolled\\n          <input name=\\"name\\" defaultValue=\\"Ada\\" />\\n        </label>\\n        <button type=\\"submit\\">Read value</button>\\n      </form>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}","checks":["Distinguish value/onChange from defaultValue and DOM-owned state.","Avoid unintended controlled/uncontrolled transitions.","Consider validation, reset, rendering and component integration."],"steps":["Controlled inputs derive their displayed value from React state; uncontrolled inputs retain current values in the DOM.","A large form also needs a coherent validation and reset model. Line count alone is not a sufficient selection criterion."],"remember":"Choose field ownership for the behavior you need.","wrong":[]},"re10":{"title":"Transitions and deferred rendering","prompt":"A large search list blocks typing. What can useTransition/useDeferredValue do, and what can they not do?","why":"Changing render priority does not remove work. Keep the input responsive and communicate pending or stale results.","pitfall":"Work priority and work quantity are different questions.","solution":"const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);\\n// The list may use the deferred query; the input retains the current query.","checks":["Separate urgent input updates from lower-priority rendering.","Do not confuse them with network debounce.","Consider algorithms, virtualization or workers for expensive work."],"steps":["Typing should immediately update the input while the list may briefly display the previous result.","These hooks schedule rendering; CPU work, DOM size and request count remain separate concerns."],"remember":"Work priority and work quantity are different questions.","wrong":[]},"re11":{"title":"Error boundaries and async failures","prompt":"Why does an Error Boundary not handle every onClick or API error?","why":"Plan both a broad fallback and local failure flows.","pitfall":"A global fallback does not replace local error handling.","solution":"try { await save(); } catch (error) { setSaveError(toUserMessage(error)); }","checks":["A boundary handles relevant descendant rendering/lifecycle errors.","Event-handler and ordinary async failures need explicit handling.","Provide recovery and avoid sensitive data in logs."],"steps":["A render failure may require a controlled fallback for part of the tree.","A rejected save action should become useful local UI state with recovery, not an assumed boundary catch."],"remember":"A global fallback does not replace local error handling.","wrong":[]},"re12":{"title":"Server and Client Components","prompt":"Distinguish RSC, SSR and hydration. Where do secrets, interactions and browser APIs belong?","why":"This is a useful supplementary track when the target role uses such a framework.","pitfall":"Distinguish execution location from the point of interactivity.","solution":"Explain: server data → serializable props → interactive component → authorized server request.","checks":["Do not equate RSC with SSR.","Use client state/events and browser APIs at appropriate times.","Keep secrets and authorization on the server; respect framework boundaries."],"steps":["SSR produces HTML on the server, hydration attaches client behavior, and RSC introduces a different component execution boundary.","Client components can also be pre-rendered on the server, so browser APIs are not automatically safe during render."],"remember":"Distinguish execution location from the point of interactivity.","wrong":[]},"re13":{"title":"Optimistic UI and rollback","prompt":"An optimistic row status update is rejected. Design success, rollback and concurrent-update behavior.","why":"Optimistic UI is a product decision; some operations benefit more from explicit server confirmation.","pitfall":"A faster-feeling UI needs a strong failure path.","solution":"Describe pending state, mutation identity, reconciliation/refetch and conflict rules.","checks":["Distinguish confirmed data from optimistic state and handle failure.","Account for rapid consecutive requests and operation identity.","Understand useOptimistic/useActionState without treating them as a complete API contract."],"steps":["The UI temporarily predicts success, so it must retain a distinction between prediction and confirmation.","A naive rollback can erase a newer change. Define operation identity and conflict handling."],"remember":"A faster-feeling UI needs a strong failure path.","wrong":[]},"re14":{"title":"Custom hooks without over-abstraction","prompt":"Pagination logic appears on three screens. When would you extract a hook, a function or a component?","why":"A useful boundary reduces repetition while retaining clarity. Sharing a hook does not inherently share a state instance.","pitfall":"Sharing implementation does not automatically share data.","solution":"Define a small API with inputs, outputs and an explicit page-reset rule.","checks":["Use a hook to share React state/effect logic.","Use a plain function for pure transformations.","Avoid a generic abstraction with many behavior flags."],"steps":["A pure calculation can be a normal function tested without React.","A custom hook helps share React behavior, but separate calls normally retain separate state."],"remember":"Sharing implementation does not automatically share data.","wrong":[]},"we01":{"title":"Button or clickable div?","prompt":"What is the best starting element for an action that opens a dialog?","why":"A native button provides semantics, focus and standard keyboard activation.","pitfall":"Start a standard action with a standard HTML element.","options":["A <button type=\\"button\\"> with a meaningful name","A <div onClick> without other behavior","An <a href=\\"#\\"> in every case","An unnamed image with a click handler"],"steps":["The browser already understands the element as an action.","A div would require you to recreate keyboard behavior and semantics. ARIA alone does not implement those interactions."],"remember":"Start a standard action with a standard HTML element.","wrong":["Mouse clicking alone does not provide keyboard operation or button semantics.","Links primarily navigate, and href=\\"#\\" can change the page position.","An unnamed image does not communicate the action or provide suitable operation."]},"we02":{"title":"An accessible dialog and form","prompt":"What do you manually check when a modal form opens?","why":"A visual screenshot cannot establish complete keyboard and assistive-technology behavior.","pitfall":"Test the full keyboard flow, including failure and exit.","solution":"Check Tab/Shift+Tab, submission, validation errors, closing, and focus restoration.","checks":["Move focus into the dialog, manage its focus flow, and return focus appropriately.","Provide an accessible name, field labels, useful errors and keyboard control.","Handle Escape and background interaction according to requirements."],"steps":["Opening a dialog changes the user’s working context, so focus must follow it.","Associate errors with fields and avoid relying only on color. Closing should restore a meaningful continuation point."],"remember":"Test the full keyboard flow, including failure and exit.","wrong":[]},"we03":{"title":"CSS layout and the cascade","prompt":"When do you choose Grid or Flexbox, and how do you debug a rule that does not apply?","why":"These are guidelines rather than absolute prohibitions. Test actual content, zoom and available width.","pitfall":"Diagnose the cascade instead of hiding it with !important.","solution":".cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }","checks":["Consider Grid for coordinated rows and columns, Flexbox for a main-axis flow.","Inspect cascade, specificity, layers, source order and computed styles.","Do not solve every conflict by adding !important."],"steps":["Choose a layout model based on the relationships between items.","Computed styles identify the winning declaration, allowing a targeted correction rather than another override."],"remember":"Diagnose the cascade instead of hiding it with !important.","wrong":[]},"we04":{"title":"Responsive design is more than media queries","prompt":"A long translated title and a table break the mobile layout. How do you investigate?","why":"Breakpoints should follow content and tasks; mobile cards should retain shared filtering and sorting rules.","pitfall":"Change layout while preserving meaning and data rules.","solution":"Check a 320 px layout, tablet, desktop, 200% zoom, and a substantially longer translation.","checks":["Test narrow screens, zoom, long text and touch controls.","Understand min-width: 0, wrapping and appropriate table scrolling.","Preserve information, order and accessibility rather than blindly hiding columns."],"steps":["Minimum content sizes, language and zoom affect layout independently of the device name.","A visually tidy mobile layout can still be wrong if it changes the information or business ordering."],"remember":"Change layout while preserving meaning and data rules.","wrong":[]},"we05":{"title":"fetch and HTTP errors","prompt":"A server responds with HTTP 404. What does fetch normally do?","why":"Receiving an unsuccessful HTTP response differs from failing to reach the server.","pitfall":"Separate network failure, HTTP status and response format.","options":["Resolves with a Response; inspect response.ok/status.","Always rejects as it would for a network failure.","Automatically converts the response to null.","Automatically retries until it receives 200."],"steps":["The browser successfully received a response even when the server reports a missing resource.","Check status according to the contract, then process the body; an error body might be HTML rather than JSON."],"remember":"Separate network failure, HTTP status and response format.","wrong":["HTTP failure statuses do not automatically reject fetch.","fetch returns a Response rather than mapping it to null.","Retries require an explicit and bounded policy."]},"we06":{"title":"401, 403 and stale requests","prompt":"When should an API response log a user out, and how do you protect a newer session from an older response?","why":"A simple interceptor can cause unwanted logouts. Request context and session identity matter.","pitfall":"Handle a status together with the request’s context.","solution":"Check whether the request used the current token, belongs to the current session, and matches the endpoint contract.","checks":["Distinguish authentication failure from insufficient permission using the API contract.","Associate 401 handling with the active authenticated request/session.","Avoid global logout for every 403, login call or stale response."],"steps":["401 usually concerns acceptable authentication; 403 concerns permission, with exact behavior determined by the API.","A previous session’s response must not blindly invalidate a newer login."],"remember":"Handle a status together with the request’s context.","wrong":[]},"we07":{"title":"XSS, CSRF and session storage","prompt":"Compare script-execution risk with automatically sent cookies. Why is there no universal token-storage answer?","why":"The design depends on architecture and threats. HttpOnly does not remove all consequences of malicious same-origin script execution.","pitfall":"One flag or token location cannot solve the entire threat model.","solution":"Begin with the threat model and API contract, then define storage, lifetime, rotation and protections.","checks":["Understand XSS execution and access to JavaScript-readable data.","Cookie authentication needs suitable CSRF protection; HttpOnly prevents JS reading the cookie.","Consider SameSite, CSP, sanitization, authorization and the actual flow."],"steps":["XSS executes unwanted code in the app’s context; CSRF exploits automatically attached credentials.","Even without reading an HttpOnly cookie, malicious code may issue requests from the app."],"remember":"One flag or token location cannot solve the entire threat model.","wrong":[]},"we08":{"title":"Frontend environment values are not secrets","prompt":"A build variable is included in JavaScript delivered to a browser. Does its prefix or .env origin hide the key?","why":"Public client identifiers can be legitimate with suitable restrictions. Actual secrets do not belong in delivered bundles.","pitfall":"Treat anything sent to a browser as accessible to its user.","options":["No. Browser-delivered data can be inspected; keep actual secrets on the server.","Yes, if its name contains SECRET.","Yes, if .env is not committed.","Yes, minification encrypts it."],"steps":["The browser must receive the JavaScript it executes, and the user can inspect that code and its requests.","A private build variable can become a literal in a public bundle; its origin does not change the delivered result."],"remember":"Treat anything sent to a browser as accessible to its user.","wrong":["A variable name creates no access-control mechanism.","Ignoring the source file in Git does not stop bundling its value.","Minification reduces code size rather than encrypting secrets."]},"we09":{"title":"Caches and stale resources","prompt":"Compare Cache-Control, ETag and a client query cache. Why might a user see an old version?","why":"Design caching and invalidation per resource type. Random cache-busting parameters are not a complete strategy.","pitfall":"Identify which cache owns the stale response before invalidating it.","solution":"Inspect response headers and the actual resource source in the Network panel first.","checks":["Separate HTTP cache, application cache and any Service Worker.","Connect ETag to conditional validation.","Consider fingerprinted assets and the document/deployment policy."],"steps":["HTTP cache rules decide whether a response can be reused or needs validation; ETag identifies a version for that validation.","Query caches and Service Workers add other layers with their own rules."],"remember":"Identify which cache owns the stale response before invalidating it.","wrong":[]},"we10":{"title":"CORS is not authorization","prompt":"Why does enabling CORS not secure an API? Can frontend code repair a server CORS policy?","why":"Non-browser clients do not obey the same CORS restrictions, so the API still needs its own protection.","pitfall":"CORS restricts browser response access; it is not server authorization.","solution":"Check origin, preflight, credentials and the relevant server headers.","checks":["CORS governs browser access to cross-origin responses.","The server enforces authentication and authorization.","Do not propose no-cors as a way to read ordinary protected JSON."],"steps":["A browser’s response-reading policy does not determine who may perform a business operation.","no-cors does not turn an otherwise inaccessible JSON response into a normally readable one."],"remember":"CORS restricts browser response access; it is not server authorization.","wrong":[]},"li01":{"title":"Where should state live?","prompt":"Classify an open modal, URL filters, authentication identity, server list and form draft.","why":"A global store for everything can obscure ownership; several overlapping stores can do the same.","pitfall":"The kind of data and its lifetime determine ownership.","solution":"Modal: local UI; shareable filter: URL; server list: query layer; draft: form; session: explicit auth boundary.","checks":["Distinguish local UI, URL, shared client and server state.","Avoid copying one server cache into several stores without a reason.","Explain ownership, lifetime and sharing requirements."],"steps":["A modal is local to its interaction, while shareable filters often belong in the URL.","A server list has freshness and invalidation rules; a form draft contains temporary user edits."],"remember":"The kind of data and its lifetime determine ownership.","wrong":[]},"li02":{"title":"Redux Toolkit and immutable updates","prompt":"Why can a createSlice reducer look mutating while directly mutating React state causes problems?","why":"Draft syntax does not permit mutating arbitrary objects anywhere in the application.","pitfall":"Understand the tool’s mechanism before copying its syntax elsewhere.","solution":"// In a createSlice reducer:\\nstate.items.push(action.payload);\\n// Outside the draft context, follow the immutable contract.","checks":["RTK uses Immer drafts in the appropriate reducer context.","Distinguish draft changes from arbitrary object mutation.","Keep reducers free of nondeterministic side effects."],"steps":["Immer records draft changes and produces an immutable result.","Outside that context, the same assignment can mutate the original object. A network request does not belong in a pure reducer."],"remember":"Understand the tool’s mechanism before copying its syntax elsewhere.","wrong":[]},"li03":{"title":"Saga takeLatest and cancellation","prompt":"A search uses takeLatest. What is cancelled, and why might network or server work continue?","why":"Cancelling a task and aborting network work are different operations; the server may already be processing.","pitfall":"A cancelled task does not prove a server operation was undone.","solution":"Explain finally/cancelled handling, the transport adapter and a server operation that still completes.","checks":["Understand cancellation of the previous Saga task.","Verify transport cancellation and signal propagation.","For mutations, consider idempotency and server state."],"steps":["takeLatest cancels the previous corresponding Saga task when a newer action arrives.","The transport and server have separate lifecycles that require explicit integration and reconciliation."],"remember":"A cancelled task does not prove a server operation was undone.","wrong":[]},"li04":{"title":"RTK Query or TanStack Query?","prompt":"What do these tools solve compared with useEffect + fetch? Justify a choice for an existing Redux app.","why":"These are useful additional topics, not a claim that every role requires both libraries.","pitfall":"Choose a clear ownership and invalidation model.","solution":"Include every relevant parameter in the key; explain stale data, refetching and invalidation after saving.","checks":["Discuss keys, invalidation, deduplication and mutations.","Choose for architecture and requirements rather than popularity.","Avoid introducing competing sources of truth."],"steps":["Server state needs request sharing, freshness and refetch rules beyond storing a response.","Existing Redux integration and team experience contribute to the choice."],"remember":"Choose a clear ownership and invalidation model.","wrong":[]},"li05":{"title":"Axios interceptors and refresh","prompt":"How do you avoid refresh loops and many parallel refresh requests?","why":"Refresh is an agreed authentication protocol rather than a universal interceptor trick.","pitfall":"Authentication retries need both a limit and session identity.","solution":"Request → relevant 401 → one refresh → at most one retry → explicit failure.","checks":["Bound retries and exclude login/refresh endpoints.","Share one active refresh promise when the protocol permits it.","Tie retries to the current session and clean up interceptors appropriately."],"steps":["Independent failures can otherwise trigger competing refresh operations.","Coordination needs bounded retries and protection against reviving an expired or replaced session."],"remember":"Authentication retries need both a limit and session identity.","wrong":[]},"li06":{"title":"Formik, RHF and server validation","prompt":"A large form uses MUI, synchronous validation and server checks. What matters before choosing or migrating a form library?","why":"Formik is relevant to the existing stack; RHF and schemas are additional options rather than automatic rewrites.","pitfall":"Define form behavior first, then choose the tool.","solution":"Define validation timing, pending/duplicate submit behavior, server errors and post-success reset.","checks":["Map field/form errors accessibly.","Consider field ownership, reset, dirty state and performance.","Keep the server authoritative and require a reason for migration."],"steps":["A form library coordinates values and submission but cannot decide what the server accepts.","Measure the specific issue and check integration, reset and errors before replacing a working form."],"remember":"Define form behavior first, then choose the tool.","wrong":[]},"li07":{"title":"Client-side table operation order","prompt":"For a complete dataset already in the browser, which order gives globally correct sorting?","why":"Sorting after slicing only changes one page. Server pagination needs a suitable server sorting contract.","pitfall":"You cannot globally sort data you have already sliced.","options":["Filter → sort the full filtered set → paginate.","Paginate → sort only the current page.","Sort only the mobile cards.","Sort formatted strings regardless of their meaning."],"steps":["Once you select ten rows, their comparator cannot see a row on another page that should come first.","Apply shared sort values, comparator and direction to the full client-side result before slicing."],"remember":"You cannot globally sort data you have already sliced.","wrong":["This only sorts within the page.","Desktop and mobile can then disagree on the business result.","Formatted dates or currencies may not sort like their underlying values."]},"li08":{"title":"react-intl and real data","prompt":"Explain plurals, dates, currencies and fallback. Why can concatenated translated fragments be wrong?","why":"Internationalization includes language rules and data formatting. Avoid calculating with already formatted text.","pitfall":"Separate a data value from its language-specific presentation.","solution":"Example message: {count, plural, one {# item} other {# items}}","checks":["Use complete messages and ICU plurals where appropriate.","Separate locale display from stored values.","Test long text, missing messages and time zones."],"steps":["Languages differ in word order and plural rules, so complete messages provide necessary context.","Store trustworthy numeric/date values and format them for display rather than treating display strings as source data."],"remember":"Separate a data value from its language-specific presentation.","wrong":[]},"li09":{"title":"Routing, loaders and error UI","prompt":"Design an admin route with URL filters, nested views, loading, errors, back/forward and permissions.","why":"React Router has different modes, so tailor the answer to the actual project configuration.","pitfall":"Test direct navigation, history, refresh and failure.","solution":"Check direct links, refresh, back, missing ids and forbidden actions.","checks":["Define URL parsing and serialization.","Use loading/error behavior appropriate to the router mode.","Client guards improve UX; server permissions remain mandatory."],"steps":["A URL should recreate shareable state through direct navigation and browser history.","Route coordination and UI guards do not prevent direct unauthorized API requests."],"remember":"Test direct navigation, history, refresh and failure.","wrong":[]},"li10":{"title":"Large tables and library choices","prompt":"100,000 rows need selection, filtering and export. What do you clarify before choosing a grid?","why":"Virtualization reduces DOM size but does not solve transfer, memory or server-query correctness.","pitfall":"Separate DOM size, transfer size and operation meaning.","solution":"Specify query parameters, count/cursor behavior, stable ids, selection semantics and export limits.","checks":["Ask about data size, permissions, latency, licensing and accessibility.","Separate DOM virtualization from server pagination/filter/sort.","Define whether selection and export cover a page or the full result."],"steps":["Rendering a small window can still leave a huge dataset in memory.","Server pagination reduces transfer, but selection and export need explicit full-result semantics."],"remember":"Separate DOM size, transfer size and operation meaning.","wrong":[]},"qa01":{"title":"Test user-visible behavior","prompt":"How do you test a user form and save confirmation without depending on internal implementation?","why":"A useful test catches broken behavior while surviving reasonable internal refactors.","pitfall":"A test should fail when the user task breaks.","solution":"Arrange a realistic response → fill fields → submit → see confirmation; add a separate server-error case.","checks":["Find controls by role/name and interact as a user.","Await meaningful async outcomes and inspect requests or visible results.","Cover validation and failure, not only success."],"steps":["A role-based interaction checks the contract that the user actually sees.","An internal-state test can pass while the real button is broken; assert the visible outcome and failure path."],"remember":"A test should fail when the user task breaks.","wrong":[]},"qa02":{"title":"Unit, integration and end-to-end tests","prompt":"Choose tests for a comparator, a form with a mock API, and a real login flow. What does each prove?","why":"Pick the fastest useful test layer for the particular risk.","pitfall":"Every layer gives specific evidence and leaves specific unknowns.","solution":"State what remains untested: actual identity provider, network, permissions and deployment configuration.","checks":["Use a focused unit test for pure logic.","Use integration tests for component interactions and a mocked contract.","Use E2E for connected flows without equating mocks with production."],"steps":["A pure comparator needs boundary examples; a form needs its interacting parts exercised.","Even E2E evidence applies to its tested environment, account and scenario. A mock cannot verify the real backend."],"remember":"Every layer gives specific evidence and leaves specific unknowns.","wrong":[]},"qa03":{"title":"Flaky E2E without sleeps","prompt":"A test sometimes fails on slower CI. How do you investigate and fix it?","why":"Flakiness can reveal either a weak test or a real application race.","pitfall":"A retry does not replace diagnosis.","solution":"Start with the failed trace and identify which required state was absent at the action.","checks":["Use traces, screenshots and network evidence.","Await meaningful state with stable locators rather than fixed sleeps.","Isolate data, timing and external dependencies; retry is not the fix."],"steps":["A sleep guesses a duration that changes across environments; a state-based wait checks the actual readiness condition.","Trace evidence determines whether the test, application, or shared environment needs correction."],"remember":"A retry does not replace diagnosis.","wrong":[]},"qa04":{"title":"Core Web Vitals","prompt":"Which group matches the core metrics verified for this training?","why":"LCP concerns main content, INP interaction responsiveness and CLS visual stability.","pitfall":"Loading, responsiveness and stability answer different questions.","options":["LCP, INP, CLS","FCP, FID, component count","TTFB, Lighthouse score, npm package count","Only bundle size"],"steps":["The metrics cover distinct parts of experience: loading, response to interaction and unexpected movement.","They do not measure every aspect of application quality. Lab evidence and field behavior have different roles."],"remember":"Loading, responsiveness and stability answer different questions.","wrong":["INP replaced FID in the core set; component count is not a Core Web Vital.","These may be tools or useful measurements, but are not that core set.","Bundle size informs analysis but does not fully describe user experience."]},"qa05":{"title":"Performance: hypothesis before optimization","prompt":"An admin screen is slow. Separate network delay, computation, DOM size and React rendering.","why":"Optimization without diagnosis often adds complexity without addressing the problem.","pitfall":"Identify the portion of duration you are actually changing.","solution":"Record the flow, duration, device, data size, trace, change and measured outcome.","checks":["Reproduce with a relevant device and dataset.","Use Network, Performance and React Profiler for distinct questions.","Compare before/after and check functional regressions."],"steps":["Memoization cannot repair a slow endpoint, and a faster server cannot alone fix a huge DOM.","Measure the same user flow before and after one targeted change to test the hypothesis."],"remember":"Identify the portion of duration you are actually changing.","wrong":[]},"qa06":{"title":"CI and build quality","prompt":"Which checks should precede a React/TS merge, and how do you scale the scope?","why":"Checks should address the actual risk rather than be identical for every change.","pitfall":"Match verification to impact and failure risk.","solution":"Define fast required checks and additional coverage for auth, data, routing and deployment changes.","checks":["Run type checking, relevant static checks and tests.","Build and exercise critical paths according to risk.","Do not equate a successful command with business or production correctness."],"steps":["Type checking, behavior tests and packaging detect different classes of failure.","Authentication or API changes require different evidence from a small visual adjustment."],"remember":"Match verification to impact and failure risk.","wrong":[]},"qa07":{"title":"Git conflicts and safe review","prompt":"Both branches change an API mapping. How do you resolve and verify the merge?","why":"A textually resolved conflict can still contain a semantic conflict.","pitfall":"A clean textual merge can still be behaviorally wrong.","solution":"Give an example where two individually correct patches fail when combined.","checks":["Read both intentions and the current API contract.","Do not blindly choose ours/theirs for the entire file.","Review the final diff and check behavior introduced by both changes."],"steps":["Git can combine valid syntax while combining incompatible assumptions.","The final diff is a new result requiring its own review and checks."],"remember":"A clean textual merge can still be behaviorally wrong.","wrong":[]},"qa08":{"title":"Dependency decisions in 2026","prompt":"How do you select a library, or justify not introducing it into an existing project?","why":"The exercise tests an enduring decision process rather than memorizing a changing package catalog.","pitfall":"A dependency is a technical decision with ongoing costs.","solution":"Compare maintaining the current solution, a small addition and a migration; record why and when to reconsider.","checks":["Start with the problem, maintenance, licensing, accessibility and compatibility.","Check current documentation and integration/bundle costs.","Do not equate stars, popularity or age with quality."],"steps":["A dependency supplies functionality and a long-term maintenance obligation.","Benefits must justify migration, regression and learning costs in the specific module."],"remember":"A dependency is a technical decision with ongoing costs.","wrong":[]},"sr01":{"title":"Design an admin module","prompt":"Design users: list, filters, details, edit form and different permissions.","why":"There is no single correct folder tree. Clear ownership and change boundaries matter more.","pitfall":"Explain ownership through a concrete scenario.","solution":"Start with user flows and contracts, then map modules and the data each owns.","checks":["Separate UI, domain, API mapping and query/state responsibilities.","Plan loading/empty/error, accessibility, i18n and direct routes.","Explain test strategy without inventing an unnecessary framework."],"steps":["Architecture should make changing user flows and contracts easier, rather than merely naming folders.","Show where data enters, who owns it, how failures become UI, and how the critical flow is verified."],"remember":"Explain ownership through a concrete scenario.","wrong":[]},"sr02":{"title":"API contracts and unknowns","prompt":"The backend is not ready. How do you build without claiming integration is already verified?","why":"Senior communication separates local evidence from unresolved backend and deployment behavior.","pitfall":"State what the evidence covers and what still requires verification.","solution":"Write: verified behavior, supporting evidence, unverified assumptions and the next check that resolves each.","checks":["Agree on schemas, status codes, pagination, permissions and failures.","Treat contract-derived mocks as frontend evidence, not real-server verification.","Track integration unknowns and plan the final checks."],"steps":["A mock returns the response you configured; it cannot establish what the real server returns.","Track actual auth, permissions, pagination, error bodies and deployment as explicit remaining checks."],"remember":"State what the evidence covers and what still requires verification.","wrong":[]},"sr03":{"title":"Risk-based code review","prompt":"A large PR changes UI and an auth interceptor. How do you prioritize and report a finding?","why":"Review quality depends on accurate, useful findings rather than comment volume.","pitfall":"A finding should support reproduction and a useful decision.","solution":"Example: an old 401 after a new login logs out the current user; demonstrate the sequence and bind handling to session identity.","checks":["Prioritize data loss, security and contract changes.","Give a concrete trigger, evidence, impact and repair direction.","Review the remaining diff after a finding and separate style from behavior."],"steps":["Limited review time should first address the highest-impact failure paths.","A reproducible trigger and consequence make the finding actionable; one bug does not clear the remaining diff."],"remember":"A finding should support reproduction and a useful decision.","wrong":[]},"sr04":{"title":"Tell a technical decision story","prompt":"Choose a real project. Explain the problem, two alternatives, decision, your role and a verifiable result.","why":"Experience duration provides context; the answer must demonstrate responsibility and decision depth.","pitfall":"A verifiable outcome beats an impressive unsupported percentage.","solution":"Situation → constraints → alternatives → decision → your contribution → result → what you would change now.","checks":["Separate personal contribution from team contribution.","Discuss real alternatives and tradeoffs.","Use actual evidence instead of invented percentages or impact."],"steps":["The interviewer needs to see your reasoning under real constraints, not only the stack name.","Separate your decisions, team work and measured outcomes. If no metric exists, say what evidence does exist."],"remember":"A verifiable outcome beats an impressive unsupported percentage.","wrong":[]},"sr05":{"title":"Mentoring without taking over","prompt":"A colleague repeatedly stores derived state with an Effect. How do you help and verify understanding?","why":"Mentoring should build independence rather than merely move authorship to the reviewer.","pitfall":"Mentoring should increase another person’s independence.","solution":"Ask which external system the Effect synchronizes with, then remove the redundant state together.","checks":["Use one concrete example and its consequence.","Ask the colleague to propose a fix and apply it to another example.","Agree on guidance and follow up respectfully."],"steps":["A concrete example connects feedback to a visible consequence.","A new example tests whether the colleague can transfer the rule beyond the corrected PR."],"remember":"Mentoring should increase another person’s independence.","wrong":[]},"sr06":{"title":"Deadlines, estimates and delegation","prompt":"A small team has an unclear feature and a short deadline. Plan without false precision.","why":"An estimate may need a range and assumptions rather than an unjustified exact promise.","pitfall":"Estimates have assumptions; plans change with evidence.","solution":"Give an example of a short spike, phased delivery and a concrete definition of done.","checks":["Separate known work, unknowns, dependencies and minimum useful scope.","Delegate coherent parts with acceptance criteria and review boundaries.","Update estimates with evidence and communicate risk early."],"steps":["A brief investigation can uncover dependencies that substantially change the estimate.","Acceptance criteria allow independent work while review boundaries protect integration."],"remember":"Estimates have assumptions; plans change with evidence.","wrong":[]},"sr07":{"title":"A production incident","prompt":"After deployment, some users cannot save a form. What do you do first?","why":"A speculative hotfix can expand an incident. Protect users and establish the actual failure path.","pitfall":"Separate reducing impact from proving the root cause.","solution":"Explain how you would check stale bundles, API contracts, permissions and duplicate submission.","checks":["Assess impact and consider rollback/feature flags within your authority.","Collect safe logs, network evidence, versions and reproduction.","Separate mitigation, cause and prevention; communicate verified facts."],"steps":["Mitigation reduces immediate user harm and buys time for diagnosis.","A permanent correction needs sufficient evidence about the cause and affected boundaries."],"remember":"Separate reducing impact from proving the root cause.","wrong":[]},"sr08":{"title":"When not to rewrite","prompt":"A team proposes a complete rewrite on a new stack. Evaluate the strongest cases fairly.","why":"Neither novelty nor familiarity settles the decision. Compare evidence and constraints.","pitfall":"A decision should survive a fair comparison of alternatives.","solution":"Write a short ADR: context, options, decision, consequences and a review trigger.","checks":["Identify a measurable problem and the cost of the current system.","Compare incremental repair, modular extraction and rewrite.","Include migration, training, regressions and success conditions."],"steps":["A rewrite may remove a genuine limitation while recreating many hidden existing behaviors.","Incremental extraction may deliver earlier benefits, but actual constraints can also justify a rewrite."],"remember":"A decision should survive a fair comparison of alternatives.","wrong":[]},"ai01":{"title":"A task for Codex or Claude Code","prompt":"Write a prompt to fix a search race. What makes the result verifiable?","why":"Useful context and verification reduce ambiguity; prompt length alone is not quality.","pitfall":"A clear goal and a verifiable outcome make a useful task.","solution":"Fix stale results in SearchPanel. Reproduce slow A arriving after B. Preserve the public API. Check both response orders and cancellation. Report checks run and remaining unknowns.","checks":["Provide the real failure, relevant files/contracts and bounded scope.","Include reproduction and acceptance criteria.","Request diff review, relevant checks and explicit unverified areas."],"steps":["An agent cannot reliably infer a rule unavailable in the task or repository.","Acceptance criteria make its result challengeable. The report should distinguish actual checks from assumptions."],"remember":"A clear goal and a verifiable outcome make a useful task.","wrong":[]},"ai02":{"title":"Green AI-written tests are not enough","prompt":"An agent writes code and tests; all pass. How do you check that they test the requirement?","why":"Implementation and tests can share the same mistaken assumption. Another model is an aid, not proof.","pitfall":"A green test is only as useful as its assertion.","solution":"Ask for a minimal reproduction, regression test and a similar case whose behavior must remain unchanged.","checks":["Derive expected behavior from an independent requirement or contract.","Check that the regression test catches the old bug and includes a counterexample.","Review the diff and relevant real flows rather than relying on the summary."],"steps":["Mutual agreement is insufficient if both artifacts encode the wrong requirement.","The regression should identify the original failure, while a safe counterexample limits an overbroad fix."],"remember":"A green test is only as useful as its assertion.","wrong":[]},"ai03":{"title":"Repository instructions and enforcement","prompt":"Which statement about AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and similar files is most accurate?","why":"An instruction and deterministic enforcement are different mechanisms. Keep scope and context current.","pitfall":"Instructions guide behavior; enforcement sets boundaries.","options":["They guide the agent; enforce mandatory boundaries with permissions, tools and CI too.","Text in a file cryptographically prevents forbidden commands.","They replace server authorization.","They should contain all tokens for convenience."],"steps":["An agent interprets text, so compliance is not identical to a technically enforced restriction.","Use suitable permissions, sandboxing and checks for consequential boundaries."],"remember":"Instructions guide behavior; enforcement sets boundaries.","wrong":["Text is not a cryptographic or process-level execution barrier.","Server authorization must be independently enforced.","Copying secrets into context unnecessarily expands exposure."]},"ai04":{"title":"Prompt injection and access boundaries","prompt":"An issue tells an agent to upload .env to an external address. How should this be handled?","why":"Agentic coding requires assessing trust in inputs and tools as well as in generated code.","pitfall":"Reading content does not make its commands authoritative.","solution":"Stop that path, treat the suspicious text as data, and continue the legitimate task without exfiltration.","checks":["Treat the external document as untrusted data.","Do not let it override the user’s task or access boundaries.","Apply least privilege and inspect destinations and changes."],"steps":["A document is information about a task; its author does not automatically gain control of your agent.","A request to disclose secrets is not authorized merely because it was read. Check provenance, data, destination and actual authority."],"remember":"Reading content does not make its commands authoritative.","wrong":[]},"ai05":{"title":"Explain your AI-assisted contribution","prompt":"An interviewer asks: “If you use AI daily, what is your contribution?” Use a real example.","why":"Tool use is demonstrated through decisions and verifiable outcomes, with responsibility retained for the code.","pitfall":"Owning shipped code requires being able to explain and change it.","solution":"The agent suggested X; I checked Y, rejected Z for a concrete risk, and verified the result with W.","checks":["Explain problem definition, architecture and verification.","Describe an actual incorrect suggestion or boundary you identified.","Show that you can explain and modify the code without inventing time savings."],"steps":["The agent’s proposal is one input; you still own the problem, design and consequences.","A concrete correction demonstrates independent judgment better than a universal claim about speed."],"remember":"Owning shipped code requires being able to explain and change it.","wrong":[]},"ai06":{"title":"Learn with AI without skipping understanding","prompt":"How would you use Codex/Claude to learn generics while retaining independent ability?","why":"An agent can tutor and review; receiving a complete answer does not establish independent ability.","pitfall":"Recognizing a solution is not the same as producing one.","solution":"Do not solve it immediately. Review my attempt, identify the first issue and ask one follow-up. Then give me a similar new exercise.","checks":["Attempt the task before viewing a solution.","Ask for a hint, explain your code and check it.","Later solve a variation without help and run type checking."],"steps":["An unaided attempt reveals the actual knowledge gap that a readable finished solution can hide.","A hint, correction and later variation create a fresh test of whether learning transfers."],"remember":"Recognizing a solution is not the same as producing one.","wrong":[]},"js13":{"title":"Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone","prompt":"What happens in this standalone example?","why":"let has block scope and a temporal dead zone. Hoisting does not mean every declaration is readable before initialization.","pitfall":"Hoisting is not permission to read every value early.","code":"console.log(value);\\nlet value = 2;","options":["It throws ReferenceError because let is read before initialization.","It prints undefined, just like any var.","It prints 2 because the whole statement is hoisted.","It prints null."],"steps":["The binding exists in its scope but cannot be read before initialization completes.","var has different initialization and scope rules; do not transfer them to let or const."],"remember":"Hoisting is not permission to read every value early.","wrong":["Some var reads before assignment produce undefined, but this example uses let.","The initializer assigning 2 has not executed.","null is not an automatic initial value for this binding."]},"js14":{"title":"Equality and type coercion","prompt":"What do these two comparisons print?","why":"== performs coercion here; === does not equate a string and a boolean.","pitfall":"Understand coercion and keep ordinary comparison contracts clear.","code":"console.log(\\"0\\" == false);\\nconsole.log(\\"0\\" === false);","options":["true, false","true, true","false, false","They throw TypeError."],"steps":["In the loose comparison, the string and boolean lead to numeric comparison with zero.","Strict equality preserves the type distinction, so the second expression is false."],"remember":"Understand coercion and keep ordinary comparison contracts clear.","wrong":["The second comparison does not perform this coercion.","The first comparison is true for these specific values.","Both comparisons are valid and do not throw this error."]},"js15":{"title":"Arguments and references","prompt":"What is the final value of a.x?","why":"The parameter receives a copy of the reference value. Mutation affects the shared object; parameter rebinding does not rebind a.","pitfall":"Copying a reference does not deeply copy its object.","code":"const a = { x: 1 };\\nfunction update(value) {\\n  value.x = 2;\\n  value = { x: 3 };\\n}\\nupdate(a);\\nconsole.log(a.x);","options":["2","3","1","JavaScript always deeply copies arguments."],"steps":["value.x = 2 modifies the object also referenced by a.","value = { x: 3 } only changes the local parameter binding."],"remember":"Copying a reference does not deeply copy its object.","wrong":["The new object is only assigned to the local parameter.","The shared object has already been mutated.","Argument passing does not imply deep cloning."]},"js16":{"title":"Pure functions and mutation","prompt":"A function returns sorted items. Which expression preserves the input order?","why":"A pure transformation avoids changing its input. Copying the array before sort protects its order, but retains element references.","pitfall":"Purity includes avoiding unintended input mutation.","options":["[...items].sort(compare)","items.sort(compare)","items.reverse()","items.splice(0), followed by using the original"],"steps":["sort mutates the array it is called on; copying gives it a separate outer array.","If you then mutate an element object, the shallow copy is insufficient to isolate that change."],"remember":"Purity includes avoiding unintended input mutation.","wrong":["This sorts the original array in place.","reverse mutates and does not provide the requested ordering.","splice changes the original and can remove its elements."]},"js17":{"title":"map, filter and reduce","prompt":"Select active products, return their names and calculate their total price. Explain each method.","why":"These methods express different transformations. Do not use map purely for side effects when its result is unused.","pitfall":"Choose the method for the result you need.","solution":"const active = products.filter(p => p.active);\\nconst names = active.map(p => p.name);\\nconst total = active.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.price, 0);","checks":["filter selects a subset without intentionally mutating the input.","map constructs a result for each selected item.","reduce includes an initial value and handles an empty array."],"steps":["filter selects, map changes shape, and reduce combines many values into one.","An initial sum of 0 specifies the empty-array result and avoids reduce’s missing-initial-value failure."],"remember":"Choose the method for the result you need.","wrong":[]},"js18":{"title":"Why does async forEach not wait?","prompt":"Items must save sequentially before success is shown. Which flow satisfies that requirement?","why":"forEach returns undefined and does not await callback promises. Use for...of for sequencing or await parallel promises when appropriate.","pitfall":"Await the promises that actually represent the work.","options":["for (const item of items) await save(item); then show success.","await items.forEach(async item => save(item)); then show success.","items.map(async item => save(item)); without awaiting results.","Show success after a fixed setTimeout delay."],"steps":["Awaiting forEach’s undefined return does not await the saves it started.","for...of with await starts each next iteration after the preceding operation. Parallel independent work needs explicit coordination instead."],"remember":"Await the promises that actually represent the work.","wrong":["forEach does not produce a promise combining its callbacks.","An unawaited promise array does not establish completion.","A fixed timer cannot establish network success."]},"js19":{"title":"Destructuring defaults and null","prompt":"What is limit after this declaration?","why":"Destructuring defaults apply to undefined, not to an explicitly supplied null.","pitfall":"A destructuring default does not replace every empty-looking value.","code":"const { limit = 10 } = { limit: null };","options":["null","10","undefined","This is invalid JavaScript."],"steps":["The input contains a present null value rather than an absent or undefined value.","If business rules treat null as missing too, use a separate normalization or ?? decision."],"remember":"A destructuring default does not replace every empty-looking value.","wrong":["10 would apply if the extracted value were undefined.","The explicit null remains the extracted value.","The destructuring declaration is valid JavaScript."]},"js20":{"title":"Prototypes and classes","prompt":"How does an object find a method it does not own? How do classes fit this model?","why":"JavaScript classes use the prototype model; methods, fields and static members do not all live on the same object.","pitfall":"Understand lookup and ownership rather than memorizing class syntax.","solution":"class User {\\n  greet() { return \\"Hello\\"; }\\n}\\nconst user = new User();\\n// greet is found on User.prototype.","checks":["Property lookup can continue along the prototype chain.","Ordinary class methods differ from instance fields and static members.","Distinguish inherited behavior from copying an entire object."],"steps":["A missing own property can be found through the object’s prototype and further along the chain.","Class syntax organizes that model without turning every member into an own instance property."],"remember":"Understand lookup and ownership rather than memorizing class syntax.","wrong":[]},"js21":{"title":"try/catch and async failures","prompt":"Why might try/catch not catch a later rejection if you return a promise without await? Show a correct flow.","why":"await within try transfers rejection into the flow catch handles. Returning a promise alone does not do that.","pitfall":"Decide whether to handle or propagate an error explicitly.","solution":"async function submit() {\\n  try {\\n    return await save();\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    report(error);\\n    throw error;\\n  }\\n}","checks":["Distinguish synchronous throws from later promise rejections.","Use await within try or an appropriate .catch handler.","Handle or propagate failures without silently hiding them."],"steps":["A try block handles failures in its execution flow, not every future rejection from a returned operation.","Awaiting inside it lets you translate or report the error and rethrow according to the contract."],"remember":"Decide whether to handle or propagate an error explicitly.","wrong":[]},"js22":{"title":"Optional chaining is not validation","prompt":"For a declared user variable, what does user?.name establish?","why":"Optional chaining handles nullish access at that point. It is not general schema validation or undeclared-variable protection.","pitfall":"Null-safe access is not full model validation.","options":["It returns undefined when user is null or undefined; it does not validate name.","It guarantees name is a string at runtime.","It converts every empty string to undefined.","It prevents ReferenceError for every undeclared name."],"steps":["The chain short-circuits on null or undefined.","It does not prove the resulting property type or declare a missing variable binding."],"remember":"Null-safe access is not full model validation.","wrong":["An untrusted property’s type still needs validation.","An empty string is not nullish.","Reading an undeclared identifier can still throw ReferenceError."]},"js23":{"title":"Recursion and a base case","prompt":"Traverse a menu tree. Explain termination, progress, deep inputs and possible cycles.","why":"Very deep input may require iteration; arbitrary graphs may require a visited set.","pitfall":"Check termination, progress and assumptions about input shape.","solution":"function collect(nodes) {\\n  return nodes.flatMap(node => [\\n    node.id, ...collect(node.children ?? [])\\n  ]);\\n}\\n// Assumes an acyclic tree of manageable depth.","checks":["Include a terminating case and move toward it.","Preserve the intended traversal order and result.","Consider stack depth and cycles when input is not guaranteed to be a tree."],"steps":["A base case returns without another recursive call, and each step must approach it.","A deep chain can exhaust the call stack; an unchecked cycle can prevent termination."],"remember":"Check termination, progress and assumptions about input shape.","wrong":[]},"js24":{"title":"Bubbling, delegation and default actions","prompt":"Explain delegation on a list. Distinguish preventDefault from stopPropagation.","why":"Stopping propagation does not inherently prevent navigation or form submission.","pitfall":"EventTarget is not guaranteed to be an Element. Check its type before calling DOM methods such as closest.","solution":"list.addEventListener(\\"click\\", event => {\\n  if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;\\n  const button = event.target.closest(\\"button[data-id]\\");\\n  if (!button || !list.contains(button)) return;\\n  openItem(button.dataset.id);\\n});","checks":["Distinguish target from currentTarget.","Use bubbling and verify the matched element within the container.","preventDefault affects the default action; stopPropagation affects propagation.","Checks that target is an Element before closest and keeps the container boundary."],"steps":["target identifies the event source, while currentTarget identifies the element running this handler.","Bubbling allows a parent handler to act on child events; cancellation and propagation are separate choices."],"remember":"preventDefault and stopPropagation solve different problems.","wrong":[]},"re15":{"title":"Rules of Hooks","prompt":"Why can useState not be called in only one conditional branch?","why":"Call ordinary Hooks in the supported top-level component/custom-Hook context. The special use API has different rules.","pitfall":"Ordinary Hooks need consistent call structure.","options":["React needs a consistent order for these Hook calls across renders.","A component is allowed only one useState.","Hooks only work in class components.","It is always valid if the condition usually stays the same."],"steps":["Conditional useState calls can change the sequence between renders of the same instance.","Move the condition into supported logic or extract a component rather than depending on a usually stable branch."],"remember":"Ordinary Hooks need consistent call structure.","wrong":["A component can have multiple useState calls.","Hooks are designed for function components and custom Hooks.","Usually stable is not a guarantee of consistent structure."]},"re16":{"title":"Pure rendering and props","prompt":"A component calls props.items.sort() during render. Why is that a problem, and how do you fix it?","why":"Rendering can repeat or be interrupted. Input mutation introduces order-dependent behavior and affects other consumers.","pitfall":"The same inputs should support predictable rendering.","solution":"const sorted = [...items].sort(compare);\\nreturn sorted.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} />);","checks":["Do not mutate props or their shared structures.","Derive output without changing another owner’s input.","Copy the array or redesign sorting; do not add an unnecessary Effect."],"steps":["sort changes the array owned outside the render.","Copying lets you calculate output without mutating that shared input; a pure transformation needs no Effect."],"remember":"The same inputs should support predictable rendering.","wrong":[]},"re17":{"title":"useReducer or several useState calls?","prompt":"A form has related edit, submit, success and reset transitions. When is a reducer useful?","why":"A reducer can clarify related transitions, while a few independent values may be simpler with useState.","pitfall":"useReducer is a local tool and does not require Redux.","solution":"function reducer(state, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \\"reset\\": return initialState;\\n    case \\"submit\\": return { ...state, status: \\"saving\\" };\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n}","checks":["Consider connected transitions and centralized update logic.","Keep the reducer a pure function of state and action.","Do not introduce a global store just because a reducer is used."],"steps":["Actions can describe coordinated transitions more clearly than many separate setters.","The reducer calculates state; network operations and previous-state mutation do not belong in it."],"remember":"useReducer is a local tool and does not require Redux.","wrong":[]},"re18":{"title":"Lifting state and a single owner","prompt":"Two sibling components update one filter. Prevent two inconsistent copies.","why":"Two copies synchronized through Effects can create extra transitions and races.","pitfall":"Give one piece of information one clear source of truth.","solution":"const [filter, setFilter] = useState(\\"\\");\\n// Parent passes filter and onFilterChange to both children.","checks":["Choose one suitable common owner.","Pass the value and an update action, or justify another shared source.","Consider URL state for shareable navigation filters."],"steps":["A single owner provides one current value and one clear place where updates apply.","Children receive the value and intent callback; choose URL/store ownership for actual sharing and lifetime requirements."],"remember":"Give one piece of information one clear source of truth.","wrong":[]},"re19":{"title":"A render is not automatically a DOM change","prompt":"A component rendered again. What necessarily follows?","why":"Distinguish rendering calculations, committing necessary changes and browser presentation.","pitfall":"A re-render is not a reconstruction of the entire page.","options":["React recalculated its output; actual DOM changes depend on the result.","Every DOM node was deleted and recreated.","The browser reloaded the document.","Every Effect ran regardless of dependencies."],"steps":["If the relevant output is unchanged, the corresponding DOM may remain unchanged.","Effects and browser layout are not synonyms for calling a component function."],"remember":"A re-render is not a reconstruction of the entire page.","wrong":["React need not recreate every DOM node.","A component render does not imply a network document reload.","Effect execution depends on its rules and dependencies."]},"re20":{"title":"Immutably update one row","prompt":"Set done: true on a state row identified by id without changing the previous array or object.","why":"Copy the changed path; there is no need to deeply clone everything.","pitfall":"Copy the necessary changed levels, not the whole application.","solution":"setItems(previous => previous.map(item =>\\n  item.id === id ? { ...item, done: true } : item\\n));","checks":["Use previous state when deriving the update from it.","Create a new array and a new object for the changed row.","Preserve other references and stable ids."],"steps":["map creates a new outer array, and only the modified row needs a new object.","A functional updater uses the appropriate prior state instead of a potentially stale captured variable."],"remember":"Copy the necessary changed levels, not the whole application.","wrong":[]},"we11":{"title":"Box model and border-box","prompt":"With width: 100px, padding: 10px, border: 2px solid and border-box, what is the total width excluding margins?","why":"border-box includes content, padding and border within the declared width. Margins remain outside.","pitfall":"Check the box model before calculating dimensions.","options":["100 px","124 px","120 px","96 px"],"steps":["The inner content gets the remaining space after padding and borders.","The declared 100 px is the total border-box width rather than a content-only width."],"remember":"Check the box model before calculating dimensions.","wrong":["124 px would be the content-box calculation: 100 + 20 + 4.","Padding is included rather than added outside the declared width.","Subtracting borders alone does not give the total border-box width."]},"we12":{"title":"absolute, relative and sticky","prompt":"Explain a badge positioned inside a card and a sticky header in a scroll container.","why":"Positioning depends on context and scroll boundaries, not just a pixel offset.","pitfall":"Always interpret positioning in its surrounding context.","solution":".card { position: relative; }\\n.badge { position: absolute; top: .5rem; right: .5rem; }\\n.header { position: sticky; top: 0; }","checks":["Understand normal flow and the absolute element’s containing block.","Use an appropriate reference container and account for transform/contain rules.","Check the sticky scroll ancestor, inset and available space."],"steps":["A relatively positioned card commonly establishes the reference for its absolute badge.","Sticky depends on the scrolling environment; transforms and other rules can also affect containing contexts."],"remember":"Always interpret positioning in its surrounding context.","wrong":[]},"we13":{"title":"Why does a flex child overflow?","prompt":"A long file name overflows a flex row. What do you check before using a fixed width?","why":"An automatic minimum can prevent shrinking. Allowing shrink and choosing text presentation are separate decisions.","pitfall":"Shrinking a box and presenting its content are separate concerns.","solution":".row { display: flex; }\\n.name { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }","checks":["Inspect content minimums, flex-basis and shrinking.","Consider min-width: 0 and suitable wrapping or ellipsis.","Keep the full content accessible when truncating."],"steps":["A flex item may refuse to shrink below a content-related automatic minimum.","min-width: 0 permits shrinking, after which wrapping or accessible truncation must be chosen deliberately."],"remember":"Shrinking a box and presenting its content are separate concerns.","wrong":[]},"we14":{"title":"Buttons inside forms","prompt":"An ordinary Cancel button is inside a form. What explicitly prevents it from submitting?","why":"Use type=\\"button\\" to specify an ordinary action rather than relying on the form button’s default submit behavior.","pitfall":"Explicitly identify which buttons submit a form.","options":["type=\\"button\\"","type=\\"submit\\"","Only name=\\"cancel\\"","Only a .cancel CSS class"],"steps":["Cancel is a separate action whose semantics should be explicit.","The name, text and CSS class do not select the button’s form behavior."],"remember":"Explicitly identify which buttons submit a form.","wrong":["submit explicitly selects form submission.","name alone does not select this button type.","A CSS class changes presentation, not native form semantics."]},"we15":{"title":"HTTP methods and idempotency","prompt":"Explain safe and idempotent semantics. Why can retrying a create POST cause duplicates?","why":"The resulting server state matters. Method semantics and the actual endpoint contract determine retry safety.","pitfall":"Check operation semantics before retrying a failure.","solution":"Example contract: send an idempotency key for a create operation; the server associates retries with the same operation.","checks":["Distinguish non-mutating intent from repeatable operation effects.","Idempotency does not require identical response bodies.","Consider server-supported idempotency keys and retry policies."],"steps":["Safe concerns intended non-mutating behavior; idempotent concerns repeated identical intent producing the same intended effect.","A connection failure can hide an already successful create; a server idempotency contract can associate its retry with that operation."],"remember":"Check operation semantics before retrying a failure.","wrong":[]},"we16":{"title":"The browser rendering pipeline","prompt":"Connect DOM/CSS, style, layout, paint and compositing. What is layout thrashing?","why":"Geometry reads after writes can force repeated layout calculation. Measure instead of relying on a universal optimization slogan.","pitfall":"Batch reads and writes, then confirm the trace.","solution":"Read required geometry first, calculate changes, then batch DOM writes. Verify the resulting trace.","checks":["Distinguish stages without assuming every change runs all of them.","Recognize alternating DOM writes and geometry reads.","Batch work and verify a Performance trace."],"steps":["The browser derives geometry and pixels from document structure and styles. Different changes need different stages.","Alternating mutations and measurements can force calculations earlier and more often than necessary."],"remember":"Batch reads and writes, then confirm the trace.","wrong":[]},"vite01":{"title":"What does Vite do in a React project?","prompt":"In a standard client-only React application, what does Vite provide during development and delivery?","options":["It executes React components on the user's phone instead of its JavaScript engine.","It provides a development server and produces production assets that a static host can serve.","It replaces the API server and automatically checks user permissions.","It turns TypeScript types into runtime validators for every network response."],"why":"Vite is a development and build tool. Browser React code still runs as JavaScript; an ordinary SPA build can be served as static content.","steps":["During development the tool processes modules and enables fast updates while you edit.","For delivery you create a build, and the host sends its HTML, JS and CSS assets to users."],"remember":"Vite prepares and serves code; the business API and browser have separate responsibilities.","pitfall":"A working development server does not prove that production APIs, paths or hosting are configured correctly.","wrong":["The user's browser still executes client JavaScript; Vite is not its JavaScript engine.","Authorization requires server rules and does not follow from the build tool.","Static types do not automatically become network validators."]},"vite02":{"title":"A VITE_ variable is not a secret vault","prompt":"You store a private API token as a protected CI variable called VITE_API_TOKEN and use it in browser code. Who can discover the value?","options":["Only a CI administrator, because the variable is marked as a secret there.","Only the repository author, provided the .env file is not committed.","A user receiving the bundle can discover it; the confidential call belongs behind an appropriate server.","Nobody, if you rename the VITE_ prefix through envPrefix."],"why":"Protecting a value in CI protects the CI store. It does not change the fact that the client build contains what the browser needs to use.","steps":["Follow the value from CI into the build and then into code delivered to the user.","Keep a confidential token on the server and apply authentication, authorization and call limits there."],"remember":"Treat anything the browser must know as accessible to the user.","pitfall":"Public identifiers or keys designed for browser use can be public; distinguish them from confidential server tokens.","wrong":["The secret label limits certain CI displays but does not protect a value embedded in JS.","Git ignore prevents committing the file, not embedding its value in a build.","Changing the prefix changes variable selection, not the confidentiality of embedded values."]},"vite03":{"title":"Transpilation is different from type checking","prompt":"vite build succeeds even though the IDE reports a TypeScript error. What do you check first in CI?","code":"const quantity: number = \\"12\\";","options":["Whether minification is enabled, since it validates assignments.","Whether the file is renamed from .ts to .tsx, since only .tsx checks types.","Whether HMR is enabled, since it fully checks production code.","Whether a separate TypeScript check, such as tsc --noEmit, runs and blocks delivery when it fails."],"why":"Vite processes TypeScript syntax for execution, but its transform does not replace whole-program type checking.","steps":["The annotation can be removed from this file without proving that the assigned value matches its declared type.","Make type checking a required step and continue to build and delivery only after it passes."],"remember":"Add an explicit TypeScript check to development and CI.","pitfall":"If npm run build already runs TypeScript before Vite, that script provides the protection, not vite build alone.","wrong":["Minification optimizes output; it does not prove TypeScript contracts.","Both extensions need type checking; .tsx additionally supports JSX syntax.","HMR is a development update mechanism, not a production type checker."]},"vite04":{"title":"What does HMR actually promise?","prompt":"Editing a module during development causes a full page reload and local form state disappears. What is the most accurate explanation?","options":["HMR attempts to apply updates through supported module boundaries; some edits still require a full reload.","Vite must permanently preserve every React state value across all source edits.","HMR only changes CSS, so React components can never update without reloading.","HMR automatically stores React state in localStorage, so lost state proves an API problem."],"why":"HMR has update-accepting boundaries and can fall back to a full reload. React Fast Refresh state preservation also depends on the edit.","steps":["Distinguish replacing a supported module from loading the entire document again.","For lost important state, inspect the development edit and module boundaries; design durable storage separately."],"remember":"HMR speeds up iteration; it is not durable data storage.","pitfall":"One development reload does not establish that the same state behavior occurs in production.","wrong":["Preserving every state value across every possible edit is not guaranteed.","Vite supports component HMR integrations with the framework's relevant rules.","HMR itself does not provide localStorage durability, and this symptom does not prove an API issue."]},"vite05":{"title":"A development proxy is not a production API contract","prompt":"fetch('/api/users') works locally with server.proxy but returns 404 after static deployment. What is the best conclusion?","options":["Add CORS: * to the React request and the browser will locate the backend.","Copy node_modules into dist so the proxy keeps running.","Check the production API URL or hosting reverse proxy; Vite server.proxy configures the development server.","Enable changeOrigin in the browser to ship the Vite proxy to users."],"why":"Relative /api/users requests target the production origin after deployment. A Vite development rule does not automatically configure a static host.","steps":["Inspect the exact production request URL and server response in the Network panel.","Configure a supported production API path or server reverse proxy, with the appropriate CORS and authentication."],"remember":"A local proxy is a development convenience; define the production network path separately.","pitfall":"CORS governs browser access to responses; it does not create a missing server route.","wrong":["Browsers do not set the server's Access-Control-Allow-Origin, and CORS does not fix missing routes.","Installed dependencies are not a static proxy service that browsers run.","changeOrigin is a server proxy option, not a browser mechanism for shipping the proxy."]},"vite06":{"title":"Imported image or public folder?","prompt":"You want an image in Vite's dependency graph and a processed build URL with a hash when emitted as a file. Which approach do you choose?","code":"// src/components/Profile.tsx","options":["Put it in public and always expect a content hash in its original filename.","Import the image from src and use the resulting URL in the component.","Write an absolute development-machine disk path in JSX.","Hardcode /src/assets/avatar.png in JSX and assume the source folder exists on the host."],"why":"An imported asset participates in the build graph. Vite can emit it with a hash or inline a small asset under the relevant configuration.","steps":["The import gives the tool a dependency it can analyze and rewrite during the build.","Use public for stable naming or copying requirements outside that processing; do not assume its files behave like hashed imports."],"remember":"Use imports for processed assets; public serves distinct asset requirements.","pitfall":"Do not rely on an exact output filename: a small imported asset may be inlined.","wrong":["Public assets retain their names when copied; that differs from imported build assets.","Your local disk path does not exist on the user's device or host.","A production build need not ship the src structure; use the generated URL."]},"vite07":{"title":"Staging mode and a production build","prompt":"You run vite build --mode staging without a separate NODE_ENV override. Which description is correct?","options":["It automatically launches the dev server and disables production optimization.","All environment values are loaded from the browser user's operating system at runtime.","MODE and PROD must have the same type and value.","It selects staging mode and its environment configuration while the build remains production by default."],"why":"Mode selects mode-specific values. NODE_ENV and mode are distinct, so staging does not imply a development bundle.","steps":["Separate an environment's purpose, such as its staging API, from build optimization mode.","Inspect the actual import.meta.env values and build command instead of inferring NODE_ENV from the mode name."],"remember":"Staging can use a production build with different public configuration.","pitfall":"An existing CI environment variable can outrank an .env file; inspect the process's real inputs.","wrong":["build still creates a build; --mode alone does not turn it into a development server.","Vite prepares the environment values; client code does not read the user's operating-system environment.","MODE is a string; PROD is a boolean related to the production environment."]},"vite08":{"title":"An old tab cannot load its lazy chunk","prompt":"A user opened the app before a deployment. Later they visit a lazy route and its old hashed chunk no longer exists. Propose deployment and recovery behavior that preserves their work.","why":"The tab already executes a specific code version and requests assets from that version. HTML cache handling and asset retention reduce the problem; controlled recovery handles remaining failures.","steps":["Match the requested URL to its entry-bundle version and a 404 in the Network panel; distinguish this from a general network outage.","Plan compatible delivery and HTML revalidation, then offer a bounded reload after saving drafts."],"remember":"Asset lifetimes must account for tabs still executing the previous version.","pitfall":"An automatic reload loop can lose input or repeat while the user is offline.","checks":["Distinguishes a missing old chunk from other network failures.","Proposes HTML revalidation and sensible versioned-asset retention.","Handles vite:preloadError or an appropriate app error boundary while preserving drafts.","Bounds retries and displays a message if recovery fails."],"solution":"Example approach: revalidate HTML (for example Cache-Control: no-cache), version hashed assets and retain the preceding deployment during transition where possible. On vite:preloadError save the draft, offer a “Load the new version” button, and allow at most one automatic attempt per version. For offline users offer retry once connectivity returns. Test in a tab opened before deployment.","wrong":[]},"vite09":{"title":"Deploying under a subdirectory","prompt":"A SPA should live under /training/, but JS/CSS requests incorrectly target the domain root. Which Vite setting do you check first?","options":["The public asset base, followed by separate checks of the router basename and hosting fallback.","The React root DOM element's name.","The browser's parallel request count.","Only server.port, because production asset URLs depend on the development port."],"why":"base controls public asset URLs. It relates to the deployment location but does not resolve all client-routing rules by itself.","steps":["Inspect generated index.html and Network requests: /assets/... differs from /training/assets/....","Align the build base with the hosting path, then test direct visits and refreshes of supported routes."],"remember":"Asset paths, router paths and hosting fallback must agree.","pitfall":"Setting base does not automatically turn an arbitrary server 404 into a SPA route.","wrong":["The DOM root identifies where rendering occurs; it does not set public build paths.","Browser concurrency does not fix incorrectly generated URLs.","The development port does not configure production static-asset paths."]},"vite10":{"title":"A lazy module still loads eagerly","prompt":"You want a large editor only on a dedicated route, but the entry module also statically imports and renders it. What is the best first step?","code":"import Editor from './HeavyEditor';\\n// elsewhere: lazy(() => import('./HeavyEditor'))","options":["Add another lazy import without changing the static dependency.","Only rename the file to HeavyEditor.lazy.tsx.","Remove the unnecessary eager dependency from the initial path, create a real async boundary and measure production output.","Disable TypeScript, since it prevents code splitting."],"why":"A lazy import can defer loading, but an initial static dependency can defeat that goal. Check the actual production build's chunk behavior.","steps":["Follow the dependency graph from the entry to the heavy editor, including barrel re-exports.","Isolate the rarely used part behind an async boundary, then compare transferred bytes and loading experience before and after."],"remember":"Deferral requires a real async boundary, not a lazy filename.","pitfall":"Code splitting adds loading steps; loading and error states belong in the solution.","wrong":["A second import does not remove the existing initial dependency.","A filename alone does not define the chunking strategy.","TypeScript is not why the static import remains an eager dependency."]},"vite11":{"title":"build.target is not a browser API catalogue","prompt":"You lower build.target, but an older supported browser lacks Array.prototype.toSorted. What do you need to consider?","options":["target automatically polyfills every missing Web and JavaScript API.","Besides syntax transformation, check runtime support and use a polyfill or compatible implementation where needed.","Changing the array's TypeScript annotation is sufficient without changing executable code.","A passing build means no additional browser check is needed."],"why":"Syntax transformation and runtime API availability are different checks. A language target does not prove that a particular method exists in a user's browser.","steps":["Separate syntax the tool can transform from methods implemented by the JavaScript engine.","Define supported browsers, inspect the APIs you use and test the needed fallback or polyfill."],"remember":"A syntax target does not guarantee every runtime capability.","pitfall":"Do not add an entire polyfill package blindly; inspect actual requirements and bundle cost.","wrong":["Vite does not provide every runtime polyfill by default, and target does not promise that.","A TypeScript annotation does not implement a missing browser method.","Generating a bundle successfully does not prove every API works on the target device."]},"vite12":{"title":"Vite 7 to 8: what does a senior check?","prompt":"Your team upgrades Vite 7 to Vite 8. A colleague says, “Just change the version; it is still esbuild and Rollup.” Explain the change and propose a verifiable upgrade plan.","why":"Vite 8 uses Rolldown and Oxc tools instead of the earlier esbuild/Rollup combination. A familiar plugin interface helps migration but does not prove identical behavior for every project.","steps":["Inventory versions, plugins, custom transforms, build options and supported browsers first.","Then compare type checks, tests and production output; check runtime behavior, route loading, assets and rollback."],"remember":"Validate a build-tool upgrade through behavior and output, using its migration guide.","pitfall":"Do not promise another project's speedup percentage; measure this project under matching conditions.","checks":["Identifies the Rolldown/Oxc change in Vite 8.","Checks the migration guide, Node support and the plugins/options actually used.","Includes type checking, tests, production build and route/asset smoke checks.","Measures this project's build and checks target browsers and rollback."],"solution":"Create a branch and record baseline build output and duration. Read the Vite 8 migration guide, check required Node and plugin versions, and adapt relevant esbuild/Rollup configuration. Run checks and compare output, source-map handling, lazy routes, CSS and deployment paths on staging. Measure the same project and approve delivery with a known rollback procedure.","wrong":[]},"cra01":{"title":"CRA in a 2026 interview","prompt":"What is the most accurate way to discuss Create React App in a November 2026 interview?","options":["It is the React team's default recommendation for every new project.","It is important legacy tooling: CRA was deprecated for new apps on 14 February 2025; maintain existing systems and plan migration according to requirements.","Applications built with CRA automatically stopped working on that date.","CRA deprecation means React and client-side rendering are deprecated too."],"why":"Deprecation changes tool-selection and maintenance advice; it does not make an existing app stop running automatically. Understanding its build and migration risks remains useful.","steps":["Separate the recommendation for new projects from the behavior of an already deployed app.","For an existing project assess support, dependencies, requirements and migration cost instead of promising an immediate rewrite without analysis."],"remember":"CRA knowledge helps legacy maintenance; new tooling choices need a current assessment.","pitfall":"Old CRA pages document legacy behavior; they do not establish a recommendation for new projects.","wrong":["The React team announced the opposite and pointed new applications toward supported alternatives.","A deprecation notice does not delete deployed JavaScript or existing websites.","One build tool's status does not deprecate React or CSR."]},"cra02":{"title":"create-react-app versus react-scripts","prompt":"In an existing CRA project before ejection, what provides its packaged start/build/test commands and configuration?","options":["A globally installed create-react-app that runs again for every build.","react-dom itself, because it also renders the UI.","The browser, which automatically downloads webpack configuration from npm.","The project's installed react-scripts package; create-react-app primarily generates the initial project."],"why":"The generator and the tooling later used by the project are different. Standard CRA scripts invoke the locally installed react-scripts package.","steps":["Inspect package.json scripts: start/build/test invoke the corresponding react-scripts commands.","For specific behavior inspect that project dependency's version and lockfile rather than a globally installed generator."],"remember":"The generator creates the project; its react-scripts dependency drives standard tooling.","pitfall":"This describes standard unejected CRA; inspect overrides or copied scripts in customized projects.","wrong":["The global generator is not what a standard project runs for every delivery.","react-dom handles rendering/hydration rather than CRA build configuration.","Build tools run in development/CI; browsers do not install webpack from npm."]},"cra03":{"title":"An environment change leaves the old API URL","prompt":"A CRA SPA has already been built and statically deployed. You change REACT_APP_API_URL only in the file-serving server's environment. Without a separate runtime-config mechanism, what happens?","options":["The embedded URL remains; changing a build-time value requires rebuilding and redeploying.","Every existing browser immediately reads the new server environment value.","The new URL appears when you rename the React state variable.","REACT_APP_ values are always confidential, so the browser cannot use an API URL."],"why":"CRA embeds selected environment values at build time. A static server then delivers the resulting files rather than automatically rewriting them.","steps":["Distinguish the build process's environment from the environment of a server returning finished JS.","If one build must serve multiple environments, design a separate public runtime configuration and check its caching and validation."],"remember":"Build-time configuration stays fixed until a different build is delivered.","pitfall":"REACT_APP_ values embedded in the bundle are public to users; runtime configuration is not a secret store.","wrong":["The browser does not automatically access the environment of the file-serving process.","A state variable name does not change the bundle's embedded content.","The prefix exposes a value to the client; it does not make it confidential."]},"cra04":{"title":"CRA proxy and production CORS","prompt":"CRA package.json defines a local API proxy. Why does that not prove the same /api request and authentication will work after deployment?","options":["Every production API must use exactly the same port as the CRA dev server.","React forbids relative URLs in production.","The proxy is development configuration; separately verify production origins, reverse proxy, CORS and cookie rules.","A browser can only send GET requests from a production build."],"why":"The development server forwards requests in its own environment. After deployment the actual topology and browser rules determine routing and credentials.","steps":["Record the application origin, API URL and whether production requests are same-origin or cross-origin.","Verify routing/proxy configuration and the authentication contract on actual hosting; local success is local evidence."],"remember":"A development proxy does not test the production networking and authentication contract.","pitfall":"Do not treat a local proxy as proof that production cookies, CORS or permissions are configured safely.","wrong":["The same port is not required; the origin/proxy/CORS architecture determines a valid route.","Relative URLs are valid; what matters is the origin and path they resolve to.","Production browsers support other HTTP methods under the relevant rules."]},"cra05":{"title":"What does eject mean?","prompt":"Your team proposes npm run eject for one build-configuration change. Which consequence is accurate?","options":["Eject only enables debug logs and is fully reversed by the next npm start.","It copies tooling configuration and dependencies into the project; the team owns maintenance, so assess the decision and Git rollback in advance.","It turns React into a server framework with automatic API routes.","It updates every dependency to a safe version and removes the need for migration."],"why":"Ejection exposes configuration previously hidden by react-scripts and increases team responsibility. CRA provides no ordinary uneject command.","steps":["Check whether a supported setting or planned migration solves the problem without taking ownership of all configuration.","If ejection is justified, make a reviewable commit and establish rollback before editing copied scripts."],"remember":"Eject transfers configuration control and maintenance responsibility to the team.","pitfall":"CRA's one-way interface does not mean Git cannot restore an earlier commit; save a known state first.","wrong":["Eject changes project tooling; npm start does not undo it.","Exposing build configuration does not create business API routes.","Ejection alone does not guarantee updated or secure dependencies."]},"cra06":{"title":"Jest/jsdom is not a Safari test","prompt":"Legacy CRA component tests pass in Jest/jsdom. What can you confidently conclude?","options":["CSS layout and touch interactions are confirmed on a physical iPhone.","Every production CORS and cookie scenario is confirmed.","The offline service worker will definitely work after private sign-in.","Those tests passed in that environment; browser-specific flows and real integration require the appropriate additional checks."],"why":"jsdom simulates part of a browser DOM environment in Node. It helps test component behavior but is neither physical Safari nor the complete production network.","steps":["State what the test actually executes: rendering, user action, mocked responses and expected outcome.","For layout, browser APIs, installation or production authentication, identify evidence from an appropriate browser/integration test."],"remember":"A test result is bounded by what the test actually exercised.","pitfall":"Do not replace a meaningful component test with an oversized mock that merely repeats the implementation.","wrong":["jsdom does not prove actual CSS rendering or physical touch-device behavior.","A mock or simulated environment does not establish real production network policy.","Jest passing does not itself test service workers or offline private sign-in."]},"cra07":{"title":"Plan a CRA to Vite migration without losing features","prompt":"You inherit a CRA admin app using React Router, Sass, tests and environment configuration. Propose a migration sequence to Vite that separates tooling changes from product behavior.","why":"A new build tool does not automatically transfer every old convention. Verify existing features through an inventory of inputs, outputs and real workflows.","steps":["Inventory build/start/test scripts, HTML entry, environment access, proxy, asset paths, Sass and routing rules.","Transfer them to the new flow, then verify the same production app through type checks, tests, a build and critical user scenarios."],"remember":"Migration transfers contracts and behavior, not just npm commands.","pitfall":"Do not rename every REACT_APP_ key to VITE_ without reviewing it: both client configurations can expose values.","checks":["Inventories CRA conventions and chooses a suitable Vite React setup.","Distinguishes process.env.REACT_APP_* from import.meta.env.VITE_* and retains secrets on the server.","Transfers entry, assets, Sass, proxy and production base/routing contracts.","Plans the test runner, type checking, CI and rollback separately.","Checks real critical flows instead of relying only on a working dev server."],"solution":"1. Save a baseline and isolate a branch. 2. Add the Vite React entry and scripts with the appropriate index.html. 3. Review and transfer public environment values, assets, Sass and proxy. 4. Align output folder and hosting/routing rules. 5. Provide test/typecheck steps; moving to a different test runner is a separate decision. 6. Compare sign-in, direct routes, forms, API requests and the build on staging before changing delivery.","wrong":[]},"cra08":{"title":"A route works through a link but refresh returns 404","prompt":"A CRA SPA uses BrowserRouter. An in-app link to /users/42 works, while direct visits and refresh return a server 404. Explain why and safely correct the hosting rule.","why":"The client router handles navigation after the app loads. A direct URL first reaches the host, which must distinguish SPA documents, static assets and API requests.","steps":["Compare navigation without a new document with a full HTTP request for /users/42.","Serve SPA index.html for permitted frontend navigation paths while APIs and real assets retain appropriate rules and status codes."],"remember":"A client router does not replace the server's initial-document routing rule.","pitfall":"A global fallback returning HTML for missing JS or API requests can hide faults and produce incorrect responses.","checks":["Explains browser-history navigation versus a full server request.","Proposes index.html fallback for frontend navigation paths.","Keeps API and static-asset requests out of an incorrect HTML fallback.","Tests direct URLs, refresh, missing assets and any subdirectory deployment."],"solution":"Configure the actual host so existing static files are served as files, /api/... reaches the API with its proper status codes, and permitted frontend navigations receive index.html. Test a direct /users/42 visit and refresh. Also request a missing .js file and API path to ensure neither returns a false HTML success. For a subdirectory, align the build's public path with the router basename. Choose rule syntax for the server version; do not copy an old Express wildcard example without checking.","wrong":[]},"node01":{"title":"The same JavaScript, a different runtime","prompt":"A colleague copies a browser module using document.querySelector into a Node script. What should you explain first?","why":"JavaScript is the language; a runtime supplies additional APIs. Plain Node has no document or DOM, while it provides server and system APIs.","pitfall":"Sharing some Web APIs, such as fetch in supported Node releases, does not provide the entire browser environment.","options":["Node adds document when a file has the .js extension.","Separate shared logic from code that depends on the DOM.","Installing TypeScript makes document available at runtime.","Every browser module automatically runs in Node."],"steps":["Identify whether the failure concerns language syntax or an environment API.","Extract pure logic into a shared module and keep DOM operations in the browser layer."],"remember":"The language is shared; available APIs depend on the runtime.","wrong":["A file extension affects loading, not DOM availability.","TypeScript declarations do not create a runtime document object.","Browser modules may depend on the DOM and other APIs unavailable in Node."]},"node02":{"title":"Why async does not fix expensive computation","prompt":"An async HTTP handler spends ten seconds in a CPU-heavy loop before its first await. What happens to other requests in the same Node process?","why":"That ordinary JavaScript loop still occupies the event loop. Declaring a function async does not move arbitrary CPU work to another thread.","pitfall":"Promise.resolve().then(heavyCalculation) also does not turn synchronous computation into parallel work.","options":["Async automatically sends every loop to the libuv thread pool.","Only that user waits because each request receives its own JavaScript thread.","Other requests suffer latency; consider limiting, partitioning or offloading CPU work to worker threads.","An await at the end retroactively frees the event loop during the loop."],"steps":["The current callback cannot return control to the event loop until the loop yields or finishes.","Measure cost, bound input and consider a controlled worker pool or separate service for expensive computation."],"remember":"Async changes how you wait; it does not parallelize every calculation.","wrong":["libuv does not take over arbitrary JavaScript loops just because a function is async.","Node does not automatically assign one JavaScript thread per HTTP request.","A later await cannot undo time already spent blocking."]},"node03":{"title":"Move file reading to an asynchronous API","prompt":"This function runs frequently while a server handles requests. Rewrite it using node:fs/promises and show where read and JSON parsing failures are handled. The path is trusted application configuration in this exercise.","why":"The Promise API lets other work proceed while I/O is pending. Reading and JSON.parse can fail, so the caller must await the result and handle rejection.","pitfall":"JSON.parse remains synchronous; a huge document requires additional limits or a streaming design.","code":"import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nfunction loadConfig(path) {\\n  return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));\\n}","solution":"import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\\n\\nasync function loadConfig(path) {\\n  const text = await readFile(path, 'utf8');\\n  return JSON.parse(text);\\n}\\n\\ntry {\\n  const config = await loadConfig('./config.json');\\n  // Validate the parsed shape before using it.\\n} catch (error) {\\n  // Handle or report the read/parse failure at this boundary.\\n  throw error;\\n}","checks":["Uses readFile from node:fs/promises with utf8.","The function is async and awaits file contents before JSON.parse.","The caller awaits and handles or propagates both I/O and parse failures.","Does not return a misleading successful empty object for every failure."],"steps":["Replace blocking reading with the Promise API: the operation still occurs, but waiting can overlap other requests.","A failure from await or JSON.parse rejects the async function's Promise; handle it at an appropriate boundary."],"remember":"Asynchronous I/O needs both awaiting and explicit failure handling.","wrong":[]},"node04":{"title":"package.json and the module system","prompt":"The nearest package.json contains \\"type\\": \\"module\\". How does Node treat an ordinary .js file in that package?","why":"Explicit type: module selects ES module semantics for that package's .js files. A .cjs extension can explicitly mark an individual file as CommonJS.","pitfall":"Prefer an explicit package module system over relying on implicit syntax detection.","options":["As an ES module; .cjs can explicitly mark a CommonJS file.","As CommonJS because only .mjs can be an ES module.","As TypeScript because module enables type checking.","The npm package name alone determines the loading mode."],"steps":["Node finds the package boundary and its type field.","Use explicit extensions for exceptions, and understand interoperability before mixing import/export with module.exports."],"remember":"The type field selects .js module semantics, not TypeScript.","wrong":["A .js file can be an ES module when the package explicitly selects that mode.","Module loading and static type checking are separate concerns.","A package name does not replace type and extension rules."]},"node05":{"title":"Relative imports in Node ESM","prompt":"You run ordinary Node ESM without a bundler or custom loader. helper.js exists beside the importing file. Which relative import is clearly correct?","why":"Node ESM requires explicit file extensions for relative and absolute specifiers. A frontend bundler may resolve imports differently, but those assumptions do not automatically carry over.","pitfall":"Vite accepting a shorter import does not prove that the same specifier works in plain Node ESM.","options":["import { help } from './helper';","import { help } from './helper/';","import { help } from 'helper.js';","import { help } from './helper.js';"],"steps":["A specifier starting with ./ refers to a relative file.","In standard Node ESM, include the full filename and .js extension; a bare package specifier means something else."],"remember":"Include the actual extension in a relative Node ESM import.","wrong":["Node ESM does not automatically append .js for this relative import.","A directory import does not replace the concrete file's full path.","Without ./ this is a bare package specifier, not the neighboring helper.js file."]},"node06":{"title":"Why did \\"false\\" enable the feature?","prompt":"The environment contains FEATURE_ENABLED=false, but the code uses if (process.env.FEATURE_ENABLED). Why does the branch run?","why":"The value is the string \\"false\\", and a non-empty string is truthy. Parse configuration explicitly, for example by comparing with \\"true\\".","pitfall":"Server environment secrets remain private only if you avoid leaking them through logs, responses or frontend bundles.","options":["Node automatically converts false to boolean true.","Environment variables always contain the value 1.","It is a non-empty string and needs explicit parsing.","Adding a boolean TypeScript annotation fixes runtime behavior."],"steps":["Separate configuration text from a JavaScript boolean value.","Define accepted values, handle missing or invalid configuration and parse it at startup."],"remember":"Environment configuration is input text; parsing and validation are your responsibility.","wrong":["Node does not perform that automatic boolean conversion.","Environment values can contain arbitrary text, not just 1.","A type annotation does not convert the runtime string."]},"node07":{"title":"Backpressure during a large download","prompt":"A fast source writes to a slow writable stream. write(chunk) returns false. What should a manual writing loop do?","why":"False signals that the internal buffer needs breathing room. Stop sending additional chunks and wait for drain; pipeline is often preferable when connecting streams.","pitfall":"False does not prove that the current chunk was lost or should immediately be sent again.","options":["Immediately retry the same chunk until write returns true.","Pause additional writes and wait for drain before continuing.","Ignore the result because streams automatically bound all memory usage.","Convert the entire file into one Buffer to avoid waiting."],"steps":["The producer is faster than the consumer, so continued writes would accumulate memory.","Respect the stream signal and handle completion and errors rather than growing buffers without a bound."],"remember":"Backpressure matches the producer's speed to the consumer's capacity.","wrong":["The chunk should not automatically be retried; duplication can corrupt output.","Continuing to write after false can keep accumulating buffered data.","One huge Buffer increases memory cost and removes streaming's advantage."]},"node08":{"title":"A callback error is outside the old try block","prompt":"try { setTimeout(() => { throw new Error('boom'); }, 0); } catch (error) { ... } Does this outer catch handle the later error?","why":"No. The outer try finishes before the timer callback runs. Handle the failure in that callback's flow or propagate it through the appropriate Promise or callback mechanism.","pitfall":"Node APIs can report failures through throw, rejected Promises, error-first callbacks or error events; check the specific API contract.","options":["No; try/catch does not remain active until a scheduled callback later executes.","Yes; try automatically tracks every callback registered inside it.","Yes, because timeout 0 runs the callback synchronously.","Yes, if we add an Error TypeScript annotation."],"steps":["The timer is registered and the surrounding synchronous block continues and finishes.","The later callback has a separate execution flow, so failure handling must connect to that flow."],"remember":"Handle an error in the flow where it actually occurs.","wrong":["Ordinary try/catch does not automatically follow callbacks scheduled for later.","A zero timeout schedules a later opportunity rather than synchronous execution.","An Error annotation does not change runtime propagation."]},"node09":{"title":"Node runs .ts: does it check types?","prompt":"You use a supported modern Node release with built-in type stripping. A script containing erasable TypeScript annotations runs. What can you conclude?","why":"Type stripping removes supported TS syntax so the code can execute. It does not perform static checking or automatically apply tsconfig options, so tsc --noEmit remains a separate check.","pitfall":"Not every TypeScript construct works in this mode; syntax requiring JavaScript generation has additional restrictions.","options":["Successful execution proves there are no TypeScript errors.","Node automatically applied every tsconfig.json setting.","Every TS transformation supported by any bundler is available.","The script can run despite incorrect types; run a separate static check."],"steps":["Separate executing JavaScript behavior from analyzing types before execution.","Use supported erasable syntax, check Node documentation and keep type checking in development and CI."],"remember":"Running a .ts file does not prove its types are correct.","wrong":["Execution follows runtime behavior and does not establish static correctness.","Built-in stripping deliberately does not read tsconfig for such transformations.","The built-in mode has a narrower scope than a full TypeScript toolchain."]},"node10":{"title":"Gracefully shutting down a Node server","prompt":"A deployment sends SIGTERM to a process serving HTTP requests and using a database. Explain an orderly shutdown sequence and what happens when work exceeds a reasonable deadline.","why":"Abrupt termination can interrupt responses and leave work unfinished. Graceful shutdown limits new work, allows active work to complete and closes resources within a final deadline.","pitfall":"Installing a signal handler does not automatically close database pools, background jobs or upgraded/WebSocket connections.","solution":"1. Mark the instance as not ready and stop accepting new HTTP work with server.close().\\n2. Allow active requests to finish within a documented deadline.\\n3. Drain or stop background work and explicitly manage upgraded connections where applicable.\\n4. Close the database pool and other resources, handle cleanup failures, then exit with an appropriate status.\\n5. If the deadline expires, log a safe diagnostic and force termination according to the deployment policy.","checks":["Stops or drains incoming requests before closing shared resources.","Accounts for active requests and closing the database pool.","Defines a deadline and a policy for stuck requests or connections.","Separates HTTP server lifecycle from background work and special connections."],"steps":["Reduce incoming work first so cleanup does not race new requests that still need the same resources.","Then wait within the budget, close resources and guarantee eventual termination if something remains stuck."],"remember":"Graceful shutdown is an ordered sequence with a time budget, not just a SIGTERM callback.","wrong":[]},"node11":{"title":"npm ci in continuous integration","prompt":"A repository contains matching package.json and package-lock.json files. Why is npm ci useful for CI installation?","why":"npm ci installs according to the lockfile and does not rewrite it to hide a mismatch. If the manifest and lockfile disagree, installation fails.","pitfall":"A lockfile improves repeatability but does not make different Node versions, operating systems or native toolchains identical.","options":["It silently upgrades the lockfile to the latest versions regardless of package.json.","It installs the locked tree and reports a manifest/lockfile mismatch.","It can only install production dependencies and never devDependencies.","It proves that dependencies contain no vulnerabilities."],"steps":["The manifest describes intent while the lockfile records concrete dependency resolutions.","CI should expose inconsistent inputs instead of silently generating a different dependency tree."],"remember":"npm ci follows the lockfile; it does not silently repair it.","wrong":["Updating versions and lockfiles belongs in a separate reviewed change.","Dev dependencies may be installed; omission depends on environment and configuration.","A successful installation is not a complete security audit."]},"node12":{"title":"Choosing a Node release in 2026","prompt":"You are preparing a production service. Which release-selection approach is strongest under documentation checked on 6 November 2026?","why":"Choose a supported Active or Maintenance LTS line, apply patch updates and verify application compatibility. 'Always use even majors only' is not a permanent policy: a different release cycle is documented starting with Node 27.","pitfall":"The highest version number and a successful local startup do not establish production support.","options":["Always use the highest version number, including Alpha, to keep the project modern.","Reject every odd-numbered major forever without reading the release schedule.","Check current LTS/EOL status and compatibility, then pin and test the selected supported version.","Keep an EOL release whenever a package-lock exists because the lock supplies runtime security fixes."],"steps":["Check the official support schedule at the time of the decision because status changes.","Then verify dependency requirements and production behavior on the selected patch release."],"remember":"Choose by current support and compatibility, not version-number parity.","wrong":["A prerelease label is not long-term production support.","A new cycle is documented from Node 27, so the old shortcut can become outdated.","A lockfile pins packages but does not maintain an EOL Node runtime."]},"express01":{"title":"JSON middleware ordering","prompt":"A POST route is registered before app.use(express.json()). A request has valid JSON and the correct Content-Type, with no earlier parser. Why is req.body not parsed inside that route?","why":"Matching middleware runs in registration order. A parser must run before the handler that consumes its result.","pitfall":"Adding a parser at the end of a file does not retroactively process a previously handled route.","options":["Every POST route automatically receives a parsed body without a parser.","Place the parser after res.json so it does not slow down the route.","Register express.json before routes that need parsed bodies.","The issue is fixed by renaming req to request."],"steps":["Trace the request from the first matching middleware to the route.","Place parsing early enough and check Content-Type, size limits and input validation."],"remember":"Middleware preparing data must run before code consuming it.","wrong":["Express does not automatically parse every body merely because the method is POST.","After sending the response it is too late to prepare input for that handler.","Parameter names do not change middleware order or behavior."]},"express02":{"title":"Middleware must continue or finish the request","prompt":"Middleware calls logRequest(req), but never sends a response or calls next(). What happens in an ordinary Express middleware chain?","why":"Express waits for middleware to finish the response or explicitly pass control. Simply returning from the function does not invoke the next middleware.","pitfall":"Avoid calling next after a final response without a clear reason; later code may try to send a second response.","options":["The request remains unfinished; call next() to continue or finish the response.","Express automatically invokes the next function when middleware returns undefined.","The next middleware runs in parallel without next().","Express automatically serializes the logRequest result as JSON."],"steps":["Separate a JavaScript function returning from an HTTP response ending.","Choose a clear path: next to continue, next(error) for failure, or end the response."],"remember":"Middleware must pass control or finish the response.","wrong":["Returning undefined does not substitute for next.","Express does not parallelize the middleware chain that way.","An ordinary middleware return value is not automatically an HTTP body."]},"express03":{"title":"params, query and body","prompt":"The route GET /users/:id receives /users/42?tab=activity. Which mapping is correct with a conventional query parser?","why":"The path parameter id is in req.params and query-string tab is in req.query. These are request inputs whose formats and allowed values must be validated.","pitfall":"A query parser can produce different shapes; do not assume every req.query.x is a trustworthy string.","options":["req.body.id is 42 and req.params.tab is activity.","req.query.id is 42 and req.body.tab is activity.","Both values are automatically in req.params.","req.params.id is \\"42\\" and req.query.tab comes from the query string."],"steps":["The :id segment belongs to the route's path pattern, so inspect params.","The portion after ? is a query string; validate it before using it in a filter or database query."],"remember":"Path → params; after ? → query; payload → body.","wrong":["The path does not place id in the body or a query field in params.","The path identifier is not a query parameter and tab is not the body.","req.params follows declared route parameters, not every URL field."]},"express04":{"title":"A JSON parser is not a validator","prompt":"After express.json(), you want to store req.body as a user profile. What is the strongest next step?","why":"The parser checks that the payload can be interpreted as JSON under its settings. That does not establish the business shape, permitted fields or permission to modify the target profile.","pitfall":"Do not trust privileged fields such as role or ownerId merely because a client supplied them.","options":["Store all of req.body because parsing already guarantees a valid profile.","Validate permitted fields and values, then separately verify identity and modification permission.","Cast req.body to Profile and immediately persist it.","Only check that req.body is not null because everything else is already safe."],"steps":["First verify structure and business constraints with runtime validation or explicit checks.","Then authorize access to the target resource on the server and build an object containing only permitted fields."],"remember":"Parsing reads the representation; validation and authorization check the contract and permission.","wrong":["Syntactically valid JSON can contain wrong types and unauthorized fields.","A TypeScript assertion does not validate network input.","A non-null value does not establish types, allowed values or access rights."]},"express05":{"title":"Express 5 and asynchronous errors","prompt":"An Express 5 async route executes const user = await loadUser(); and then sends a response. loadUser rejects. What does Express 5 do?","why":"The async handler returns a Promise, and Express 5 automatically forwards its rejection into the error-middleware flow. This applies to the Promise the handler actually returns or awaits.","pitfall":"Detached, unawaited Promises and timer callback errors are not automatically part of that returned Promise. For legacy Express 4, check the wrapper or catch(next) pattern.","options":["It always sends 200 with an empty user object.","It automatically retries until the database responds.","It forwards rejection to the error-middleware flow, similarly to next(error).","It requires a separate setTimeout around every await."],"steps":["An async function returns a Promise that Express 5 can observe.","When await throws because of rejection, the handler's Promise rejects and Express enters error handling."],"remember":"Express 5 observes the returned Promise; detached work needs its own error path.","wrong":["Rejection is not a successful result and does not automatically become 200.","Express does not introduce retries merely because a handler is async.","A timer is unnecessary and can make error propagation harder."]},"express06":{"title":"How does Express recognize error middleware?","prompt":"Which function signature should a central Express error middleware use when registered after routes?","why":"Express identifies the special error flow by four arguments: err, req, res, next. Preserve that signature even when a simple handler does not use next.","pitfall":"Error middleware registered before routes can miss errors coming from later in the chain.","options":["(err, req, res, next) => { ... }","(req, res, next) => { ... }","(err, res, next) => { ... }","() => Promise.reject(new Error())"],"steps":["Ordinary middleware receives req, res, next; error middleware adds err first.","Register it after the routes it should protect and send exactly one appropriate response."],"remember":"Central Express error middleware takes four arguments.","wrong":["That is ordinary middleware, not the special error-handler signature.","Three arguments do not follow Express's error-middleware contract.","Creating another rejected Promise does not define a handler for existing failures."]},"express07":{"title":"A safe response for an unexpected failure","prompt":"Write error middleware after routes for an unexpected server failure. req.id is already a server-generated request ID and logger is available. Retain server diagnostics without returning stack traces or internal messages. Handle headersSent too.","why":"Clients need a stable failure response and a support reference; detailed diagnostics belong in controlled server logs. Once headers have been sent, another JSON response is impossible, so delegate the error.","pitfall":"Redact secrets and personal data in real logging systems; moving everything into a log is not protection by itself.","solution":"app.use((err, req, res, next) => {\\n  logger.error({ requestId: req.id, error: err });\\n  if (res.headersSent) return next(err);\\n  return res.status(500).json({\\n    error: 'Internal server error',\\n    requestId: req.id,\\n  });\\n});","checks":["The handler is after routes and uses the err, req, res, next signature.","The headersSent branch calls next(err) without attempting another response.","Unexpected errors receive 500, a generic message and the request ID.","Internal details stay out of the response; controlled logging records the request ID in the headersSent branch too."],"steps":["First determine whether a new HTTP response can still be formed safely.","Separate the public error contract from internal diagnostics and ensure only one response is sent."],"remember":"Keep public failures stable and internal diagnostics controlled.","wrong":[]},"express08":{"title":"CORS is not API access control","prompt":"An API allows CORS only for your frontend origin. Does this alone prevent an attacker from calling a private route using curl?","why":"No. CORS middleware sets response headers that browsers use to decide whether JavaScript may read a response. Non-browser clients do not use this as authentication or authorization.","pitfall":"Some requests can still be sent when browser JavaScript cannot read the response; CORS also does not replace CSRF considerations with cookie authentication.","options":["Yes, the server rejects every network packet not sent by that browser.","Yes, Origin is irrefutable proof of the user's identity.","Yes, if express.json is registered before CORS.","No; the private route must still verify identity and access permission."],"steps":["Separate a browser's response-reading policy from a server's decision to perform an operation.","Authenticate and authorize access to each private resource; configure CORS as a separate browser mechanism."],"remember":"CORS governs browser response access, not identity and permission.","wrong":["CORS is not a network firewall verifying the source of every packet.","Origin is not a verified user identity and other clients can set it.","A JSON parser does not turn CORS into authorization."]},"express09":{"title":"Why did the error handler miss a 404?","prompt":"No route responds to /missing and the central error middleware does not run. How do you normally define a JSON 404 response?","why":"The absence of a matching route is not automatically a thrown Express error. Ordinary middleware after routes can send a 404 before the final error handler.","pitfall":"A catch-all 404 placed before API routes can stop requests before they reach their handlers.","options":["Error middleware must automatically receive every missing route, so Express is broken.","Add ordinary final middleware after routes that sends res.status(404).json(...).","Redirect all unknown paths to 200 so browsers never report an error.","Move central error middleware before every route."],"steps":["If the chain did not respond and no error occurred, provide a normal fallback response.","Put the 404 fallback after available routes and keep error middleware for actual failures."],"remember":"A 404 fallback and error middleware have different responsibilities.","wrong":["A missing route does not have to produce an Error object.","200 would incorrectly claim successful handling of the requested API resource.","Moving error middleware does not create a conventional 404 fallback."]},"express10":{"title":"Before taking an API to production","prompt":"A small Express API uses cookie login, accepts user input and exposes an account-update route. Identify priority pre-production controls and explain what each protects. An exhaustive security checklist is not required.","why":"A production API needs multiple layers: protected transport, validated input, secure sessions and resource authentication/authorization. Connect controls to the actual flow and risk rather than merely installing middleware.","pitfall":"Helmet, CORS or a passing frontend test alone does not establish API security. Cookie settings and CSRF protection depend on the login and cross-site model.","solution":"Prioritize the account-change flow:\\n1. HTTPS and an explicit trusted-proxy deployment configuration where needed.\\n2. Validate and allowlist request fields, apply appropriate body limits, and reject unsupported input.\\n3. Verify the authenticated session and authorization for the target account on the server.\\n4. Configure session cookies for the deployment (Secure, HttpOnly and deliberate SameSite), and address CSRF for cookie-based state changes.\\n5. Protect login against brute force, keep dependencies supported and configure appropriate security headers.\\n6. Avoid leaking secrets or stack traces, log useful redacted events, and test both allowed and denied requests.\\nExact settings and additional controls depend on the architecture and threat model.","checks":["Connects HTTPS and session/cookie settings to the deployment.","Separates validation, authentication and target-account authorization.","Considers CSRF in cookie flows and limits input or login abuse.","Proposes allowed/denied-path verification without claiming one middleware solves everything."],"steps":["Start with the account-changing operation: who the user is, which account they may change and which fields are permitted.","Then protect transport and sessions, limit abuse and verify that failure and denial paths follow the contract."],"remember":"Each control should protect a specific part of the flow and have a verifiable outcome.","wrong":[]},"sass01":{"title":"Sass and SCSS: two syntaxes, one language","prompt":"A CV lists SCSS/Sass. Which explanation of the difference is correct?","options":["SCSS runs in the browser, while Sass runs on the server for each request.","SCSS is a Sass syntax: .scss uses braces, while .sass uses indentation.","SCSS has variables, but the .sass syntax does not.","SCSS is a React library that replaces CSS."],"why":"Sass provides SCSS and indented syntax for styles that compile to CSS. The syntax choice does not change the browser’s role.","pitfall":"The file extension does not imply different core capabilities: both syntaxes support Sass features.","steps":["A .scss file uses { } blocks and semicolons, making it familiar to CSS authors.","A .sass file uses indentation. In either case, the browser normally receives generated CSS."],"remember":"SCSS is Sass syntax, not a separate runtime.","wrong":["Both syntaxes compile to CSS; this browser/server distinction is incorrect.","Both syntaxes support Sass variables and other language features.","SCSS can be used with or without React; it is not a React library."]},"sass02":{"title":"A Sass variable or a CSS custom property?","prompt":"A user switches themes in a running app without another build. Which approach supports changing colors in the browser?","options":["Changing $surface in a Sass source file that has already been compiled.","Adding !default to $surface alone guarantees a change in the open browser.","CSS custom properties such as --surface, with values selected by a data-theme attribute.","Every React render automatically reruns the Sass compiler."],"why":"Sass variables are evaluated during compilation. CSS custom properties remain in CSS, where the browser resolves them through the cascade and theme rules.","pitfall":"Sass can organize tokens, but a runtime theme switch needs CSS or JavaScript that affects the rendered page.","steps":["After the build, $surface is no longer a variable that the browser can update.","If .card uses background: var(--surface), switching the theme can change --surface and trigger style recalculation."],"remember":"$token is build-time; --token can be runtime.","wrong":["Editing source without recompiling and delivering it does not change loaded CSS.","!default controls initial Sass configuration, not browser state.","A React render does not inherently run a build tool or Sass compiler."]},"sass03":{"title":"Reassigning a Sass variable does not rewrite earlier CSS","prompt":"Which width values appear in the generated CSS for .first and .second?","code":"$space: 8px;\\n.first { width: $space; }\\n$space: 16px;\\n.second { width: $space; }","options":[".first: 8px; .second: 16px.","Both get 16px because the final declaration changes earlier uses.","Both get 8px because a Sass variable can never change.","The build must fail because the variable is declared twice."],"why":"Sass evaluates variables as compilation proceeds. A later assignment affects subsequent uses, while the earlier declaration has already received its value.","pitfall":"Do not automatically apply the CSS cascade or Less lazy-evaluation model to Sass variables.","steps":["When .first is processed, $space is 8px and that value goes into its CSS.","The 16px assignment occurs before .second is processed, so the second selector gets 16px."],"remember":"A Sass assignment does not rewrite CSS already processed.","wrong":["Sass variables are not retroactive; earlier declarations retain the value used when processed.","A Sass variable can be reassigned; it is not an immutable constant.","Reassignment in this example is allowed."]},"sass04":{"title":"@use: a namespace and one module instance","prompt":"Several Sass files use the same tokens module. What holds for @use within one compilation?","options":["Every @use must duplicate all the module’s CSS in the output.","Module variables automatically become global in every project file.","@use fetches CSS over the network on every React render.","The module loads once; members use a namespace, and each file explicitly loads its own dependencies."],"why":"The module system exposes members to the stylesheet that loads them. Namespaces show where names come from, while loading a module once avoids accidental repetition of its CSS.","pitfall":"“Once” applies within one compilation. Separate Sass compilations can still produce overlapping styles.","steps":["After @use \\"tokens\\", a member can be referenced as tokens.$spacing.","Another file needs its own @use to access that member; the same module is not emitted again as a fresh copy in the same compilation."],"remember":"@use provides explicit namespaces and one load per compilation.","wrong":["@use specifically avoids reloading the same module within a compilation.","Members loaded through @use are not automatically visible in other stylesheets.","@use is a Sass build instruction, not a React or browser networking API."]},"sass05":{"title":"@forward and a style library’s public API","prompt":"You are creating index.scss to expose tokens and mixins from several files. Which description of @forward is correct?","options":["It automatically puts every forwarded variable in local scope without @use.","It exposes public members to consumers; using those members locally in the forwarding file also requires @use.","It only supports CSS selectors, never variables or mixins.","It sends Sass source from a browser to a server."],"why":"@forward creates a shared library entry point. Exposing members to its consumers does not also import those members into the forwarding file’s own scope.","pitfall":"If a module needs both @forward and @use for the same dependency, account for initial configuration; @forward usually comes first.","steps":["index.scss can forward several modules so consumers need one @use path.","If index.scss itself computes a value using a forwarded token, it must also load that module through @use."],"remember":"@forward exposes an API; @use loads it for use.","wrong":["Exposing public members is different from making them locally available.","@forward supports variables, functions and mixins as well as styles.","This organizes modules during Sass compilation; it does not transfer data between browser and server."]},"sass06":{"title":"A mixin or a function?","prompt":"You need a reusable spacing calculation inside a padding value. Which approach fits?","options":["@include can be used directly as a number inside a padding value.","A Sass @function must return an entire CSS selector, never a single value.","A Sass @function returning a value with @return; a mixin is better suited to including declaration blocks.","Mixins and functions are identical and both are only called with @include."],"why":"A function produces a value for an expression, such as spacing(3). A mixin is included in a styling context and can contain declarations or other Sass statements.","pitfall":"Avoid a complex function when simple multiplication or CSS calc() communicates the intent clearly.","code":"@function spacing($step) { @return $step * 4px; }\\n.card { padding: spacing(3); }","steps":["spacing(3) returns 12px, giving padding: 12px.","For several repeated declarations, such as border and background together, a mixin can be included with @include."],"remember":"A function returns a value; a mixin includes styles.","wrong":["@include is an at-rule, not a numeric result that fits inside a padding expression.","Sass functions return Sass values through @return.","A function is called in an expression; @include invokes a mixin."]},"sass07":{"title":"!default and library configuration","prompt":"A library declares $radius: 8px !default. What does this mean for a configurable module?","options":["It prevents $radius from ever being configured from another file.","It forces the browser to update the value when a user changes themes.","It assigns a default only when the variable is undefined or null, allowing configuration on the module’s first @use load.","It gives module selectors the same precedence as CSS !important."],"why":"!default allows configuration before the module generates styles. For example: @use \\"library\\" with ($radius: 12px).","pitfall":"Configuration belongs to the initial module load; reconfiguring an already loaded module is not a runtime theme mechanism.","steps":["The library provides a reasonable fallback, while the consumer can supply 12px when loading it.","Declarations built from the token are computed during compilation without creating a special browser variable."],"remember":"!default is a build configuration contract, not CSS precedence.","wrong":["The flag enables configuration rather than locking the token against it.","Sass configuration does not automatically run when the browser theme changes.","!default controls Sass variable assignment and does not behave like CSS !important."]},"sass08":{"title":"Why use math.div instead of relying on /?","prompt":"How can you clearly divide 24px by 3 in Sass without relying on deprecated slash-division interpretation?","options":["Use 24px // 3 because Sass follows JavaScript integer-division syntax.","Load @use \\"sass:math\\" and use math.div(24px, 3), yielding 8px.","Remove px from every expression or the calculation is invalid.","Replace every CSS / with math.div, including slash separators."],"why":"math.div explicitly expresses Sass division. CSS also uses / as a separator, so replacing every slash blindly can change a declaration’s meaning.","pitfall":"CSS calc(24px / 3) is another valid way to express a CSS calculation; Sass slash deprecation does not remove all CSS slash syntax.","steps":["Load the built-in math module; math.div(24px, 3) evaluates to a px length.","Identify slash separators separately, such as those in grid-area or color syntax; they do not represent Sass division."],"remember":"math.div clarifies Sass arithmetic; / can be a CSS separator.","wrong":["// starts an SCSS comment; it is not a division operator.","Dividing a length by a number can validly yield a length, here 8px.","A slash separator in CSS grammar is not the same as arithmetic division."]},"sass09":{"title":"How should you handle Sass @import in 2026?","prompt":"You maintain a Dart Sass 1.x project with Sass @import deprecation warnings. What is a reasonable response?","options":["Claim that every CSS @import is forbidden in browsers and delete all such declarations.","Hide every warning and assume migration will never be necessary.","Replace the text @import with @use everywhere without checking variables or mixins.","Plan an @use/@forward migration and check namespaces and generated CSS; deprecated does not mean already removed."],"why":"Sass @import has been deprecated since Dart Sass 1.80.0. The module system changes visibility and loading rules, so migration requires behavioral checks.","pitfall":"Distinguish Sass imports processed by the compiler from plain CSS @import. Do not present planned removal as already completed.","steps":["Find direct and indirect imports and identify the origin of members each stylesheet uses.","After migration, verify the build and generated CSS: namespaces, ordering and repeated output can matter."],"remember":"Deprecated signals a migration direction, not automatic current failure.","wrong":["The Sass deprecation does not prohibit standard browser CSS @import.","Suppressing warnings does not remove the obsolete dependency.","@use does not globally expose every member; a text-only replacement is often insufficient."]},"sass10":{"title":"Flatten a selector and preserve keyboard focus","prompt":"The button’s SCSS depends on an exact chain of parent elements. Propose shallow rules using .action-button with hover and focus-visible states, without !important. Explain why changing the HTML layout then affects the component less.","code":".dashboard {\\n  .panel {\\n    .toolbar {\\n      .action-button { &:hover { background: #0056ad; } }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}","why":"Nesting generates combined CSS selectors. Depending on several ancestors couples button behavior to the DOM and makes overrides harder.","pitfall":"A shallow selector is not permission to use generic global names. Choose a meaningful component class and inspect the existing cascade.","steps":["Separate toolbar layout styles from button styles because they have different responsibilities.","Use & for states on the standalone class and inspect the resulting .action-button:hover and .action-button:focus-visible CSS."],"remember":"Nesting organizes source; the generated selector determines actual coupling.","checks":["The button has a standalone .action-button class without required dashboard/panel parents.","Hover and focus-visible styles are explicit, and the focus indicator is preserved.","The response explains specificity/coupling changes and proposes checking existing styles."],"solution":".toolbar { display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; }\\n.action-button {\\n  background: #0066cc;\\n  color: white;\\n  &:hover { background: #0056ad; }\\n  &:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #0066cc; outline-offset: 3px; }\\n}\\n\\n/* The button can now move to another container. Inspect computed styles and existing overrides, because reduced specificity can change which declaration wins. */","wrong":[]},"less01":{"title":"What does the browser receive from a Less build?","prompt":"What is a reasonable way to deliver Less styles in a production app?","options":["Serve .less as ordinary CSS and expect every browser to natively understand all Less features.","Compile .less to CSS during the build and serve CSS; Less.js browser compilation exists but is not required.","Run the Less compiler on every user click.","Less can only be compiled on a PHP server."],"why":"Less extends CSS and tooling converts it into CSS. Official documentation recommends precompiling for production performance and reliability.","pitfall":"Browser compilation does exist through Less.js; that is different from native CSS-parser support for Less.","steps":["The build processes Less variables and mixins to produce CSS.","The browser loads that CSS without repeating Less compilation for each interaction."],"remember":"Deliver CSS in production; keep the preprocessor in the build.","wrong":["A different file extension does not make a native CSS parser understand Less.","Already generated CSS does not need recompilation on every click.","Less.js supports Node.js and other integrations; PHP is not its only environment."]},"less02":{"title":"Less lazy evaluation and local scope","prompt":"Which width value does .tile receive in this Less example?","code":".tile {\\n  width: @size;\\n  @size: 12px;\\n}\\n@size: 24px;","options":["24px: the final declaration in the entire file always wins regardless of scope.","The build must fail because @size is used before its local declaration.","12px: the local @size value can be resolved even though it is declared after its use.","The generated CSS keeps width as the literal @size."],"why":"Less supports lazy evaluation of variables. Here, the local scope contains @size: 12px, so .tile uses that value.","pitfall":"Lazy evaluation does not eliminate scope. Do not apply Sass’s assignment-order model to Less automatically.","steps":["Find the scope where @size is resolved: width is inside .tile.","That block has a local 12px declaration, so the outer 24px declaration does not determine this result."],"remember":"In Less, check scope and lazy evaluation, not just line order.","wrong":["An outer declaration does not automatically override a local variable.","Less variables do not have to be written before their use in this example.","A preprocessor variable is resolved during compilation rather than kept as an @size CSS value."]},"less03":{"title":"A Less mixin with a guard","prompt":"What happens when this mixin is called with .pad(3)?","code":".pad(@n) when (@n > 0) { padding: (@n * 4px); }\\n.card { .pad(3); }","options":["The guard matches, so .card receives padding: 12px during compilation.","The guard is checked in the browser when the window size changes.","Padding becomes 3px because the guard removes the multiplication.","when can only inspect the user’s login status."],"why":"A guard selects a Less mixin definition based on its argument. The number 3 satisfies @n > 0, and the multiplication yields 12px.","pitfall":"A guard is not a runtime if for React state or access rights; it is evaluated while Less generates CSS.","steps":["Calling .pad(3) binds @n to 3, making 3 > 0 true.","The mixin body is included in .card, with padding calculated as 3 × 4px = 12px."],"remember":"A Less guard selects styles from arguments during compilation.","wrong":["This guard is not connected to resize events and is not reevaluated in the browser.","The condition does not remove the expression; the body multiplies 3 by 4px.","Guards test Less expressions; they do not authorize application users."]},"less04":{"title":"Less 4 and parenthesized division","prompt":"You use Less 4 with math: \\"parens-division\\". Which declaration compiles to width: 4px?","options":["width: 12px / 3; without parentheses, because division is always evaluated immediately.","width: 12px // 3;","width: divide(12px / 3); because Less requires a built-in divide function.","width: (12px / 3);"],"why":"In this math mode, / outside parentheses remains a separator rather than performing division. Parentheses clearly identify the arithmetic Less should evaluate.","pitfall":"State the version and math mode when analyzing legacy Less. Different configuration can change arithmetic evaluation.","steps":["The question explicitly uses parens-division, the Less 4 default math mode.","The parenthesized expression evaluates 12px ÷ 3 to 4px; a preserved slash expression is not necessarily valid for a given CSS property."],"remember":"Parenthesize Less division in parens-division mode.","wrong":["Without parentheses, / is not evaluated as division in the stated mode.","// is a comment, not a Less integer-division operator.","No invented divide function is required; a parenthesized expression is sufficient."]},"less05":{"title":"&:hover does not select a hovered descendant","prompt":"Which selectors does this Less code generate?","code":".button {\\n  &:hover { opacity: 0.8; }\\n  .icon { width: 16px; }\\n}","options":[".button :hover and .button.icon.",".button:hover and .button .icon.",".button:hover and a global .icon only.","Only one selector: .button:hover.icon."],"why":"& represents the parent selector and can add a state to that same element. A normal nested .icon produces a descendant selector.","pitfall":"Selector whitespace changes meaning: .button:hover means the button is hovered, while .button :hover can select a hovered descendant.","steps":["Replace & with .button to get .button:hover without whitespace.","The .icon rule has no &, so normal nesting produces .button .icon."],"remember":"& changes selector composition; inspect the resulting CSS.","wrong":["The first selector incorrectly adds a descendant, while the second incorrectly requires both classes on one element.","A nested .icon remains scoped by the parent rather than automatically becoming global.","Two separate nested rules do not automatically become one combined selector."]},"less06":{"title":"Add a runtime theme to an existing Less project","prompt":"An existing Less token @surface: #fff produces a white card background. Propose a small light/dark theme switched through a data-theme attribute, without running the Less compiler in the browser. Explain what remains a build responsibility.","code":"@surface: #fff;\\n.card { background: @surface; }","why":"The existing Less build can continue preparing CSS, while values that change at runtime become CSS custom properties. The browser then resolves them through rules for the active theme.","pitfall":"Changing @surface in a source file does not update CSS already delivered. Check text contrast in both themes, not only the background.","steps":["Move changing colors into --surface and --text declarations for the default and dark themes.","The component consumes var(...), while the app changes data-theme on an appropriate ancestor. Less still runs at build time."],"remember":"The preprocessor prepares CSS; custom properties enable runtime themes.","checks":["Light and dark custom-property values exist, and .card consumes them with var().","Changing data-theme affects the card without a new browser Less build.","The response explains the build/runtime boundary and proposes checking colors and contrast in both themes."],"solution":":root { --surface: #fff; --text: #171717; }\\n[data-theme=\\"dark\\"] { --surface: #171717; --text: #f5f5f5; }\\n.card { background: var(--surface); color: var(--text); }\\n\\n/* These rules can live in precompiled .less source. React or another UI layer sets data-theme=\\"dark\\" on the html element, and the browser resolves the custom properties and styles. */","wrong":[]},"ui01":{"title":"One MUI theme instead of many exceptions","prompt":"Ten admin screens use different blues and spacing. How would you introduce a consistent MUI theme with less duplication?","why":"ThemeProvider supplies a theme to descendants. Shared palette, spacing, typography and component overrides centralize design rules.","steps":["Turn repeated values into semantic tokens such as primary and text.secondary.","Apply the theme above its consumers; keep genuine exceptions in local sx and check light/dark contrast."],"remember":"A theme defines shared rules; an exception stays local.","pitfall":"Scattered global !important rules can fix one screen while breaking another.","checks":["Places createTheme and ThemeProvider above consumers.","Separates tokens/component overrides from local exceptions.","Checks contrast, focus and both themes on representative controls."],"solution":"Create a theme with palette, typography, spacing and components.MuiButton.defaultProps/styleOverrides. Wrap the app in ThemeProvider. Use semantic sx values such as color: \\"text.secondary\\" on individual cards. Review forms, dialogs and disabled/focus states in both themes.","wrong":[]},"ui02":{"title":"An sx number does not always mean pixels","prompt":"With MUI default 8 px spacing, what does sx={{ margin: 2 }} mean?","why":"Spacing properties in sx use theme.spacing. On the default scale, 2 means 2 × 8 px = 16 px.","steps":["margin is connected to the spacing system.","The theme converts 2 into CSS; a custom spacing function can change that result."],"remember":"Check whether an sx number is transformed by the theme.","pitfall":"Not every numeric sx property uses the same scale.","options":["A 2 px margin.","A 16 px margin.","A 2% margin.","Two CSS classes without a margin."],"wrong":["That matches ordinary numeric React style.margin, not this theme-aware sx rule.","A number alone is not a percentage; use an explicit string for that.","sx produces styles; this number participates in the spacing calculation."]},"ui03":{"title":"Autocomplete has two distinct states","prompt":"A user types a city name without selecting a suggestion. Which MUI Autocomplete states should you distinguish?","why":"value represents the selected value; inputValue represents textbox text. Typing is different from confirming an option.","steps":["Typing \\"Lon\\" can change inputValue while value stays null.","Give onInputChange and onChange separate responsibilities."],"remember":"Search text and a selected entity are different data.","pitfall":"Do not submit a city entity just because similar text appears in the input.","options":["Only value, because every character automatically selects an entity.","Only open, because it stores both text and city.","value/onChange for selection and inputValue/onInputChange for text.","Write options directly into the DOM input."],"wrong":["Text can be incomplete and need not match a valid option.","open describes the popup, not the selected entity.","That bypasses the controlled React/MUI contract and complicates synchronization."]},"ui04":{"title":"Same ID, new reference in Autocomplete","prompt":"Options are {id, name} objects. Refetching creates new instances of the same entities. Without getOptionValue or freeSolo, how can you compare an option with value reliably?","why":"Default object equality depends on references. isOptionEqualToValue can compare a stable ID when refetching creates new objects.","code":"isOptionEqualToValue={(option, value) => option.id === value.id}","steps":["Distinct object instances can have the same ID while failing reference equality.","Compare stable IDs to express domain identity rather than instance identity."],"remember":"An entity is identified by its stable key, not its object address.","pitfall":"Names need not be unique; use the actual identifier.","options":["Use === and expect all object fields to be compared.","Compare only the index in the refreshed list.","Always serialize the selection into inputValue.","Use isOptionEqualToValue to compare IDs."],"wrong":["For objects, === compares references rather than structure.","Sorting or a new response can change the order.","That mixes search text with the selected entity and does not fix option equality."]},"ui05":{"title":"Bootstrap mobile-first columns","prompt":"In a standard Bootstrap 5.3 grid, an element uses className=\\"col-12 col-md-6\\". Without other overrides, how does it behave below and from md?","why":"col-12 occupies all 12 columns. At the default md width of 768 px, col-md-6 changes it to 6 of 12. A breakpoint rule continues upward until another rule overrides it.","steps":["Below md, the base col-12 rule applies.","From md onward, the min-width rule gives half the row width."],"remember":"A mobile-first class sets the base; a breakpoint enhances it.","pitfall":"md describes viewport width, not detection of a particular device.","options":["Full width below md; half the row from md upward.","Half below md; full width above it.","Always exactly 6 px wide.","Native browser CSS Grid with no Bootstrap stylesheet."],"wrong":["That reverses the mobile-first direction.","The number describes a share of a 12-column grid, not pixels.","These classes get their meaning from Bootstrap CSS, not built-in HTML commands."]},"ui06":{"title":"Who owns a Bootstrap dialog DOM?","prompt":"React state closes a dialog while a Bootstrap JS plugin mutates the same DOM. What is a reasonable integration direction?","why":"Two independent owners of one element can become inconsistent. Bootstrap CSS works with React; coordinate interaction through React components or a clearly isolated integration.","steps":["Choose one source of truth for open/closed state and lifecycle.","Check dialog focus, Escape and cleanup as well as visual closing."],"remember":"Give each UI element one clear behavior owner.","pitfall":"Adding another click listener does not solve unclear state ownership.","options":["Bootstrap CSS is therefore forbidden in React.","Coordinate behavior through a React integration with clear state ownership.","Keep adding direct DOM mutations until a test passes.","Set opacity: 0 and ignore focus."],"wrong":["The issue is competing DOM behavior, not simply using CSS classes.","That increases conflict between state and the actual DOM.","Invisible interactive content may remain keyboard-accessible and break accessibility."]},"ui07":{"title":"Figma CSS starts a discussion","prompt":"You receive a desktop Figma frame and generated CSS for a form. How do you turn it into a maintainable responsive React screen?","why":"Inspect values describe a design but do not fully define narrower layouts, real content or errors. Implementation still needs layout, component and state decisions.","steps":["Extract tokens, relationships and existing components before copying coordinates.","Clarify mobile, loading, empty, error and focus states; test longer text and keyboard use."],"remember":"Carry design intent and rules into code, then verify behavior.","pitfall":"Many absolute coordinates can match one screenshot and fail with a longer translation.","checks":["Distinguishes inspect references from a finished architecture.","Chooses grid/flex from relationships and reuses tokens/components.","Identifies missing states and concrete responsive/accessibility checks."],"solution":"Map spacing, typography and color tokens to the existing theme. Split the form into meaningful components and use flow/grid/flex according to structure. Clarify collapsing columns, error copy and disabled/loading states with the designer. Compare multiple widths using longer translations, a keyboard and realistic data.","wrong":[]},"ui08":{"title":"Design token or copied color?","prompt":"Figma uses a semantic text/secondary variable with different light/dark values. What best preserves that intent in the application?","why":"A semantic token connects a color role to the active theme. Copying only the current hex value loses that relationship and complicates changes.","steps":["The text role remains the same when the theme changes.","Map an application token to agreed values and check contrast in both modes."],"remember":"A token names a purpose; the theme selects a value.","pitfall":"Matching token names alone do not guarantee matching values or contrast.","options":["Copy only its light hex value everywhere.","Store the text screenshot as an image.","Detect the theme from the Figma file URL name.","Map it to a semantic application theme token."],"wrong":["The dark theme then loses its intended representation.","That loses real text, scaling and accessibility.","A file name does not define the contract between tokens and application mode."]},"viz01":{"title":"A Chart.js instance has a lifecycle","prompt":"A React component directly creates new Chart(canvas, config) in an Effect. What should cleanup do before reusing the canvas?","why":"destroy() releases the instance references and listeners. React removing a canvas is not an explicit destruction of the Chart.js instance.","steps":["Keep the instance created by this particular Effect.","Return cleanup that calls its destroy(); development Strict Mode helps expose incorrectly paired setup/cleanup."],"remember":"Release imperative resources that you create.","pitfall":"This concerns the direct Chart.js API; a React wrapper may already manage that lifecycle.","options":["Call destroy() on the created instance.","Only empty the labels array in React state.","Create another instance over the previous one without cleanup.","Call chart.update() instead of destroy()."],"wrong":["That does not remove instance listeners and references.","Destroy the previous chart before reusing its canvas for another instance.","update redraws an existing instance rather than releasing it."]},"viz02":{"title":"Changed chart data and redraw","prompt":"You changed dataset values on a direct Chart.js instance and want the new data displayed without animation. What do you do?","why":"The instance needs an update to process changes and redraw. The none mode disables animation for that update.","steps":["Change instance data, not an arbitrary React state object.","Call chart.update(\\"none\\") to process scales, data and rendering without this animation."],"remember":"Instance data plus update produces the new chart.","pitfall":"An imperative instance API does not permit mutating React state directly.","options":["Only edit an ordinary array and expect every mutation to be detected.","Call chart.update(\\"none\\").","Delete the canvas and lose the instance.","Call React setState with the same mutated reference."],"wrong":["A direct instance receives an explicit update; an ordinary array is not a redraw signal.","This update does not require destroying and rebuilding the entire chart.","The question concerns a Chart.js instance; a mutated React reference is not a reliable render signal."]},"viz03":{"title":"A chart that can also be read","prompt":"A dashboard shows monthly revenue only as a canvas chart. How would you make it understandable to a screen reader user?","why":"Canvas pixels do not by themselves expose chart data to screen readers. Provide an accessible name and a meaningful textual representation.","steps":["Summarize the chart without relying only on color.","Offer a table or another text equivalent of actual values, kept consistent with chart filters."],"remember":"A visual trend also needs an understandable text path.","pitfall":"aria-label=\\"Chart\\" names the shape without explaining the data or its meaning.","checks":["Gives the canvas an appropriate role and name.","Makes the same data available as text or a table.","Distinguishes series beyond color and verifies values/filters."],"solution":"Give the canvas role=\\"img\\" and a descriptive name such as \\"Monthly revenue for the selected year\\". Add a short trend summary and an accessible month/revenue table. Update the table and chart from the same data source. Clearly label series and check keyboard and screen reader use.","wrong":[]},"viz04":{"title":"Load Google Maps when needed","prompt":"Only one route contains a map. Which official-loader approach requests the required Maps libraries on demand?","why":"Dynamic library import lets importLibrary request a particular library when used. A shared loader avoids uncontrolled duplicate script loading.","steps":["Separate loading the API from creating an individual map.","Await the required library and show loading/error states before using its classes."],"remember":"Asynchronous API loading belongs to the UI lifecycle.","pitfall":"Do not assume google.maps exists during the first render.","options":["Append a new script tag on every render.","Use classes before loading finishes and hide the error.","Put a browser key in a VITE_ variable so the map no longer needs a network.","Use a shared loader and await google.maps.importLibrary for required libraries."],"wrong":["Rendering can repeat many times, producing duplicate and hard-to-manage loading.","Hiding an error does not make the API ready; await the Promise and handle failure.","Build configuration does not embed the Google Maps service and libraries into your app."]},"viz05":{"title":"Browser Maps keys and restrictions","prompt":"The browser Maps JavaScript API uses a key visible in a network request. What protection suits this kind of key?","why":"A browser key is not a server secret. Configure website/application and API restrictions in Google Cloud and monitor usage.","steps":["Assume users can inspect everything their browser sends.","Restrict where and for which Maps APIs the key is usable; keep server credentials on the server."],"remember":"Control browser-key usage instead of relying on concealment.","pitfall":"A VITE_ prefix and minification do not turn a browser-used key into a server secret.","options":["Restrict permitted websites and APIs and monitor usage.","Only minify the bundle.","Put it in VITE_MAPS_KEY and treat it as secret.","Use one unrestricted key for browser and server APIs."],"wrong":["Minification does not control access to a key used by the browser.","Client-exposed Vite environment values can be included in delivered code.","That broadens allowed use and couples different security boundaries to one key."]},"viz06":{"title":"Migrating the old Maps Marker","prompt":"For a new integration you choose AdvancedMarkerElement instead of deprecated google.maps.Marker. Which extra requirements matter in the migration guide?","why":"Advanced markers require the marker library and a map ID alongside the new class. Renaming a constructor alone is not the whole migration.","steps":["Check that the existing loader loads the marker library.","Provide the appropriate mapId and adapt marker creation to the new API."],"remember":"Migration includes prerequisites and behavior, not just renaming.","pitfall":"Deprecated does not mean the old Marker has already been removed from every supported integration.","options":["Only change the color of the old Marker.","Load the marker library and configure a map ID with the new class.","Remove the API key so advanced markers run locally.","Conclude the entire Maps JavaScript API is discontinued."],"wrong":["Styling an old instance does not migrate its API.","The new marker class does not remove Maps JavaScript API prerequisites.","The deprecation concerns the old Marker class, not the entire service."]},"li11":{"title":"Redux state and derived data","prompt":"A store contains a task list and a filter. Must filteredTasks be another list stored in Redux?","why":"A filtered list can be derived from tasks and the filter. Keeping multiple copies of one fact creates extra synchronization rules.","steps":["Identify minimal source data: tasks and filter.","Derive the view through a selector; memoize when calculation cost or reference stability benefits."],"remember":"Store source data and derive what follows from it.","pitfall":"A memoized selector cannot fix incorrect mutation of its inputs.","options":["Yes; every filter combination must have separate state.","No, because filtering belongs exclusively in a reducer.","No; derive it through a selector from the list and filter.","Yes, because a selector may only read one field."],"wrong":["That creates many copies which can become inconsistent.","A selector can read and derive data without changing state.","Selectors can combine and transform multiple inputs."]},"li12":{"title":"Why does catch miss a rejected thunk?","prompt":"A createAsyncThunk thunk ends with a rejected action. How can a component use try/catch around its result?","why":"Dispatch normally returns a Promise containing the final fulfilled/rejected action. unwrap returns the successful payload or throws the error/rejected payload to the caller.","code":"try {\\n  const saved = await dispatch(saveItem(values)).unwrap();\\n} catch (error) {\\n  // Show the save error.\\n}","steps":["Distinguish a rejected Redux action from rejection of the awaited Promise.","unwrap converts the action result into the success/throw contract needed by this caller."],"remember":"Use unwrap for payload-or-throw semantics.","pitfall":"This is the createAsyncThunk contract, not a rule for every custom thunk.","options":["Just await dispatch(thunk(args)); a rejected action always throws.","Catch works only after disabling Redux middleware.","Read the error payload from the toast DOM.","Use await dispatch(thunk(args)).unwrap() inside try/catch."],"wrong":["A standard createAsyncThunk dispatch Promise resolves to its final action.","Middleware enables thunk execution; removing it does not solve result handling.","A DOM message is presentation rather than a reliable request result."]},"li13":{"title":"Axios: error response or no response?","prompt":"With default validateStatus, a server sends an HTTP 422 response. Where does AxiosError normally carry that HTTP response?","why":"For an unsuccessful HTTP status, AxiosError has response with status and data. Receiving no response is different from receiving a server 422.","steps":["First identify whether the error is an Axios error.","If error.response exists, handle its status and expected validation format; otherwise distinguish networking/cancellation from request setup errors."],"remember":"An unsuccessful HTTP response differs from no response.","pitfall":"A custom validateStatus can change which statuses reject.","options":["In error.response, such as response.status and response.data.","Only in error.request; response never exists for 422.","Axios always treats every HTTP status as successful.","Always as automatically populated Formik fields."],"wrong":["A request without response commonly describes failure to receive an answer, unlike this case.","Default validateStatus does not accept every status, though it can be customized.","Axios does not know your form; the application maps server errors."]},"li14":{"title":"Cancel an Axios search","prompt":"Each query change sends an Axios request. Write an Effect that cancels the previous request, ignores stale results, and does not show cancellation as a network failure.","why":"AbortController cancels supported transport, while local activity guards UI against a result whose Effect is no longer current. Cancellation differs from server failure.","steps":["Create a controller for this Effect and pass its signal to Axios.","During cleanup invalidate its result and abort; handle cancellation separately from real errors."],"remember":"Protect both the request and its result’s right to update UI.","pitfall":"Cancelling a browser request does not guarantee the server has not already performed a write.","checks":["Passes a fresh signal per Effect and aborts in cleanup.","A late result cannot update UI after query change/unmount.","Distinguishes cancellation from failure and clears the old error on a new search."],"solution":"useEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n  let active = true;\\n  setError(null);\\n  axios.get(\\"/api/items\\", { params: { q: query }, signal: controller.signal })\\n    .then(({ data }) => { if (active) setItems(data); })\\n    .catch(error => {\\n      if (active && !axios.isCancel(error)) setError(\\"Search failed\\");\\n    });\\n  return () => { active = false; controller.abort(); };\\n}, [query]);","wrong":[]},"li15":{"title":"Formik and data loaded after mount","prompt":"A form mounts empty and later receives different initialValues. What is true about enableReinitialize?","why":"It defaults to false. When true, a deep change to initialValues can reset the form, so unsaved edits need an explicit policy.","steps":["Separate initial form setup from later reset behavior.","For refetching or record changes, decide whether incoming data may replace dirty input."],"remember":"Reinitialization can reset user input, making it a UX decision.","pitfall":"Do not enable the flag merely to show loaded data without considering a dirty form.","options":["It is always true and never affects user input.","A new reference always resets the form even with false.","True enables resetting on changed initialValues; consider unsaved edits.","It automatically saves every edit to the server."],"wrong":["The default is false, and resetting can change current values.","Formik does not unconditionally reset on every new object reference.","The flag controls local reinitialization, not a persistence API."]},"li16":{"title":"Saga call and fork","prompt":"A generator uses yield call(loadProfile). What is the key difference from yield fork(loadProfile)?","why":"call waits for the invoked function/Promise in that saga. fork starts a task without waiting for completion and returns a Task that can later be joined or cancelled.","steps":["With call, the next line waits for that invocation’s result.","With fork, the saga continues, so result dependencies need explicit coordination."],"remember":"call waits for a result; fork gives you a concurrent task.","pitfall":"Concurrent here does not mean CPU-heavy JavaScript automatically moves to another thread.","options":["fork automatically executes JavaScript on a separate CPU thread.","call changes Redux state while fork reads it.","There is no difference for asynchronous work.","call waits; fork returns a Task without waiting for completion."],"wrong":["Saga coordinates asynchronous tasks rather than implicitly creating Worker threads.","Those are not reducer/select contracts for these effects.","Waiting behavior changes ordering and coordination of subsequent steps."]},"qa09":{"title":"Undoing an already shared commit","prompt":"A regular commit on shared main introduces a regression. You want a new commit reversing its change while preserving published history. Which Git operation fits?","why":"git revert creates a new commit with an inverse change. History retains both the original change and its reversal.","steps":["Identify the exact commit and check later changes depending on it.","Apply the revert, resolve any conflicts and verify behavior before pushing."],"remember":"Use revert for a new reversing change in shared history.","pitfall":"Reverting merge commits has additional mainline semantics; this example is a regular commit.","options":["git revert <commit>.","git reset --hard followed by a mandatory force push for everyone.","git stash permanently reverses a published commit.","Just delete the local branch."],"wrong":["That rewrites shared history, contrary to the goal.","Stash primarily stores local changes rather than creating the requested reversing commit.","Removing a branch reference does not reverse main’s content."]},"qa10":{"title":"From a vague ticket to a verifiable agreement","prompt":"A Jira/Trello ticket says \\"fix search\\", with decisions scattered across Slack. As lead, what do you clarify and where do you record the agreement for implementation and review?","why":"Testable acceptance criteria connect intent with concrete behavior. A shared place for decisions reduces different interpretations during implementation, review and verification.","steps":["Turn the request into examples: user input, expected results and behavior when data is empty or the API fails.","Link the agreement, design and API contract to the ticket; record later decisions there rather than only in chat."],"remember":"An agreement should be understandable and testable without its conversation’s author.","pitfall":"Acceptance criteria describe this task; they differ from the team’s general Definition of Done.","checks":["Clarifies behavior and edge cases with stakeholders.","Writes measurable criteria linked to design/API agreements.","Preserves decision history and PR evidence without declaring one particular tool mandatory."],"solution":"Example criteria: an empty query restores the initial list; a new query shows loading; the latest query determines results; empty and error states differ; keyboard search works on mobile and desktop. Agree when requests run and how pagination behaves. Record criteria in the ticket, link the specification/Confluence, and have the PR show verification. Move important Slack decisions into the durable agreement.","wrong":[]}}`),r=[{title:`Types describe values`,tag:`Foundations`,explain:`TypeScript adds static checks to JavaScript. Runtime behavior still follows JavaScript rules. A number annotation neither converts text into a number nor validates an API response.`,mental:`An annotation is a source-code contract; conversion and validation are runtime operations.`,code:`let count = 3; // inference: number
count = 4;     // valid
// count = "4"; // type error

function double(value: number): number {
  return value * 2;
}`,trap:`Avoid mechanical annotations everywhere. Write explicit types where they clarify a boundary or inference needs help.`},{title:`Objects, optional data and unions`,tag:`Models`,explain:`Object types describe properties. ? permits a property to be absent. A | B allows either member; until you narrow it, use operations safe for the possibilities.`,mental:`If data may be missing, its type should say so honestly.`,code:`type User = {
  id: string;
  name?: string;
};
function displayName(user: User) {
  return user.name ?? "No name";
}
type Id = string | number;`,trap:`any removes protection and ! is not a runtime check. Understand unknown, undefined and strictNullChecks first.`},{title:`Narrowing and meaningful states`,tag:`Safe branches`,explain:`A JavaScript condition such as typeof or a literal status check narrows the static type. For complex state, separate union members often model valid combinations better than many optional fields.`,mental:`Identify the branch, then use only the data that belongs to it.`,code:`type Result<T> =
  | { status: "success"; data: T }
  | { status: "error"; message: string };

function read(result: Result<string>) {
  if (result.status === "success") {
    return result.data.toUpperCase();
  }
  return result.message;
}`,trap:`typeof null is "object". Validate null, property presence and property types when checking unknown objects.`},{title:`Generics preserve relationships`,tag:`Reuse`,explain:`T is a place for a concrete type supplied or inferred at a call. Unlike any, it preserves relationships between inputs and outputs. A constraint describes the minimum implementation requirements.`,mental:`T tracks the same unknown-but-specific type throughout a contract.`,code:`function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
const n = first([10, 20]); // number | undefined

function get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}`,trap:`Do not promise T if you only return its minimal constraint. Empty arrays and absent values still need honest contracts.`},{title:`Compose types without acrobatics`,tag:`Advanced foundations`,explain:`Pick selects properties, Omit excludes them, Partial makes them optional and Readonly restricts writes through that type. keyof and T[K] connect keys to values; mapped and conditional types automate useful transformations.`,mental:`Start with a sound domain model, then derive the types you actually need.`,code:`type Profile = { id: string; name: string; phone: string };
type Update = Partial<Pick<Profile, "name" | "phone">>;

const modes = ["view", "edit"] as const;
type Mode = typeof modes[number];

const config = { pageSize: 20 } satisfies { pageSize: number };`,trap:`as const and readonly do not freeze runtime objects. satisfies is not a validator.`},{title:`TypeScript you use in React`,tag:`Practical React`,explain:`Start with props and callbacks. useState often infers a useful type, but an initial null or empty array can require an explicit generic. Use the concrete DOM element when typing events.`,mental:`A component is a function with a clear input contract. Types should make incorrect usage visible.`,code:`type Props = { onSave: (name: string) => void };

function NameForm({ onSave }: Props) {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  return <input value={name}
    onChange={e => setName(e.currentTarget.value)} />;
}
// For initial null: useState<User | null>(null)`,trap:`input.value is still a string for a numeric input. ReactNode is broader than a JSX element.`},{title:`System boundaries and tooling`,tag:`APIs and checks`,explain:`External JSON is not protected by your static model. Check HTTP, parse data and validate its schema at the boundary. Building, type checking and behavior tests have different jobs.`,mental:`A type describes a promise; a validator checks whether an external value fulfills it.`,code:`const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  name: z.string()
});
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

const raw: unknown = await response.json();
const user = UserSchema.parse(raw);`,trap:`Examples assume suitable imports and configuration. parse can throw and needs a meaningful failure flow.`},{title:`Apply it to an admin screen`,tag:`Capstone`,explain:`Connect models, typed services, request states and components. Build one small vertical feature: a user list with filtering and explicit loading, error and empty states.`,mental:`Write and explain a small complete flow, then change it when the requirement changes.`,code:`type User = { id: string; name: string };
type Column<T> = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  render: (row: T) => React.ReactNode;
};
// Add service, validation, cancellation and UI states.`,trap:`Use your editor with tsc --noEmit. This training stores and displays code but does not compile or automatically grade it.`}],i={version:1,topics:t,questions:n,lessons:r};export{i as default,r as lessons,n as questions,t as topics,e as version};